latestRelease.body:
## Highlights

### MCP Events (poll/stream/webhooks + Studio inspection)
Experimental MCP Events (SEP-3415 draft) let MCP servers publish event streams that clients can poll, keep a stream open for, or receive via signed webhooks. Core/server/client add inspection APIs so Studio can list event types, stream occurrences, and test-emit events under `mastra dev`.

### Observability Upgrades: Span Aggregation + “Time to First Chunk” + Richer Trace/Span Querying
New `aggregateSpans()` storage contract enables per-span analytics (e.g., p95 latency per tool, token/cost per agent) via the `span-aggregate` feature. Observability queries gain `timeToFirstChunkMs` fields, filtering, and sorting, plus expanded trace-scope fields (root `entityId`, root `name`, root `spanType`) and richer `querySpans()` context/parent/root-entity filters (with updated store capabilities to detect support).

### Agent Controller Sessions Move to Session IDs (Multi-session per resource)
Sessions are now addressed by `sessionId` (not `resourceId + scope`) across core, server, and client-js, enabling many sessions per resource while keeping `resourceId` as the memory/thread owner key. New APIs include `controller.getSession(sessionId)`, updated `createSession()`/`deleteSession()` signatures, and server routes under `/agent-controller/:controllerId/sessions/:sessionId/...`.

### Sandboxes Get a Native Filesystem API (Faster workspace operations)
`WorkspaceSandbox` can expose an optional `filesystem` for one-roundtrip reads/writes/listings instead of shelling out per operation. This lands across providers (e.g., E2B/PlatformSandbox) and is adopted by `SandboxFilesystem` in `@mastra/code-sdk` for faster file operations.

### End-User Scoped Connect Integrations + Teams Channel
`@mastra/connect` adds per-resource (end-user) connection scoping for tools/credentials, preventing one user’s connections from serving another user’s requests, plus `userConnectRoutes()` to let authenticated end users connect their own accounts. Connect also adds Microsoft Teams as a supported channel.

### Breaking Changes
- Agent controller sessions are keyed by `sessionId` (scope removed): core/controller APIs, server routes, and client-js `controller.session()`/`session.create()` signatures updated.
- Thread-row query contract changed: use `timeRange.traces` / `timeRange.threads` instead of `traces.timeRange`.
- `querySpans()` contract changed: removed `model`/`provider` filters and dropped `cost`, `inputTruncated`, `outputTruncated`, `errorTruncated` from responses (requires updated storage adapters).
- Channel `resolveResourceId` / `resolveThreadId` contexts now require `requestContext` (tests/manual calls must pass it).

## Changelog

### [@mastra/core@1.79.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/core@1.79.0/packages/core/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- **Breaking:** Agent controller sessions are now addressed by their session id. Many sessions can share one `resourceId`, so `resourceId` only says whose threads and memory a session works on. (#27336)

  - Added `controller.getSession(sessionId)`. Removed `controller.getSessionByResource(resourceId, scope)`.
  - Removed the `scope` option from `createSession()`. Give each session its own `id` instead. When `id` is omitted it defaults to the `resourceId`, so existing one-session-per-resource code keeps working.
  - `deleteSession()` takes `{ sessionId }`, and `generateThreadTitle()` takes an optional `sessionId`.
  - The controller request context exposes `sessionId` next to `resourceId`.

  ```ts
  // Before
  await controller.createSession({ resourceId: 'repo-1', scope: 'run-a' });
  const session = await controller.getSessionByResource('repo-1', 'run-a');
  await controller.deleteSession({ resourceId: 'repo-1', scope: 'run-a' });

  // After
  await controller.createSession({ id: 'run-a', resourceId: 'repo-1' });
  const session = await controller.getSession('run-a');
  await controller.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'run-a' });
  ```

  `createSession` throws when an explicit `resourceId` doesn't match the resource of the live session with that id, so a session id can't be used to take over another resource's session.

- `aggregateTraces()` can now group by the root span's `entityId`, and trace queries and aggregates accept `entityId` as a trace-scope `where` field. Use it to split traces that have no `entityName` (for example, scorer runs recorded before scorers set one) by the primitive that produced them, then drill into a group by filtering on the same `entityId`. (#27684)

  ```ts
  const { rows } = await client.aggregateTraces({
    timeRange,
    where: { op: 'notExists', path: 'entityName' },
    groupBy: ['entityId'],
    measures: ['count'],
  });
  ```

  Trace-scope `entityId` reads the root span only. Inside `spans.some` and `spans.none`, `entityId` still matches any span in the trace. Stores that support the trace-scope field declare the new `trace-query-root-entity-id` storage feature.

  _Also in: @mastra/clickhouse@1.26.0, @mastra/duckdb@1.15.0, @mastra/pg@1.32.0_

- Added the storage contract for span aggregation. Storage adapters can implement `aggregateSpans()` and advertise the `span-aggregate` feature to answer questions like "p95 latency per tool" or "token spend per calling agent" over individual spans. (#27669)

  ```ts
  class MyObservabilityStore extends ObservabilityStorage {
    getFeatures(): readonly ObservabilityStorageFeature[] {
      return [...(super.getFeatures() ?? []), 'span-aggregate'];
    }

    // `plan` is already validated: tool calls grouped by name, with count and p95 duration
    async aggregateSpans(plan: TrustedSpanAggregatePlan): Promise<SpanAggregateResponse> {
      // e.g. { rows: [{ dimensions: { name: 'search' }, measures: { count: 42, 'duration.p95': 812 } }], truncated: false }
    }
  }
  ```

  Span aggregates use the same request shape, measures, and limits as trace aggregates. Filters follow `querySpans()`, so counts match the spans `querySpans()` returns. Spans can be grouped by name, span type, and their own, parent, or root entity. Grouping by model or provider is not supported yet. Token and cost measures count usage on the span that recorded it. Adapters without the feature reject the call as not implemented.

- Fixed `DurableAgent.observe(runId)` when attaching to a run from another process. (#26906)

  - Restored the agent's configured output processors, request context, and `returnScorerData` setting, including when the observing process also runs durable steps.
  - Preserved live call-time processors, request context, and scorer data when another wrapper observes the run within the same Mastra instance.
  - **Behavior change:** Cross-process `DurableAgent.observe(runId)` now throws `DURABLE_AGENT_OBSERVE_AGENT_MISMATCH` when the persisted run belongs to an agent with a different ID, matching `resume()` and `recover()`. Use the same agent ID in the observing process as in the process that started the run.
  - Made durable stream `onChunk` callbacks run serially and in order.
  - Made `onFinish` wait for pending `onChunk` callbacks to finish, without waiting for transport acknowledgements.

  **Before:** An observing process could use a different agent ID to attach to a run started by `researcher`:

  ```ts
  const observer = createDurableAgent({
    agent: new Agent({ ...agentConfig, id: 'researcher-observer' }),
    pubsub,
  });
  // After registering observer with Mastra and the shared storage:
  await observer.observe(runId);
  ```

  **After:** Use the starting agent's ID in the observing process. The previous configuration now throws `DURABLE_AGENT_OBSERVE_AGENT_MISMATCH`:

  ```ts
  const observer = createDurableAgent({
    agent: new Agent({ ...agentConfig, id: 'researcher' }),
    pubsub,
  });
  // After registering observer with Mastra and the shared storage:
  await observer.observe(runId);
  ```

- Sandboxes can now expose their own disk through an optional `filesystem` property on `WorkspaceSandbox`. A provider with a file API answers reads, writes, and listings in one request each instead of a shell command per step. Paths are absolute inside the sandbox, and the provider decides containment. Fall back to `executeCommand()` when the property is absent: (#27859)

  ```ts
  const pkg = sandbox.filesystem
    ? await sandbox.filesystem.readFile('/workspace/app/package.json', { encoding: 'utf-8' })
    : (await sandbox.executeCommand?.('cat', ['/workspace/app/package.json']))?.stdout;
  ```

- Added batch experiment ID filtering to observability metric queries, so you can get token usage and cost for many experiments in one request. (#27255)

  ```ts
  const result = await client.getMetricBreakdown({
    name: ['mastra_model_total_input_tokens', 'mastra_model_total_output_tokens'],
    aggregation: 'sum',
    groupBy: ['experimentId', 'name'],
    filters: { experimentIds: ['experiment-1', 'experiment-2'] },
  });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/clickhouse@1.26.0, @mastra/client-js@1.56.0, @mastra/duckdb@1.15.0, @mastra/pg@1.32.0, @mastra/spanner@1.10.0_

- `session.thread.transferOwnership` now accepts an optional `threadId`, so you can transfer a thread without switching the session to it first. Without `threadId` it still transfers the active thread. (#27043)

  ```ts
  await session.thread.transferOwnership({ toOwnerId: 'user-456', threadId: 'thread-123' });
  ```

- Channel `resolveResourceId` and `resolveThreadId` hooks now receive the per-message `requestContext`. Values a channel handler sets before calling `defaultHandler`, such as a tenant id, can be read directly in the resolvers instead of being looked up a second time. (#27436)

  ```ts
  channels: {
    handlers: {
      onDirectMessage: async (thread, message, defaultHandler, ctx) => {
        ctx.requestContext.set('tenantId', await lookupTenant(thread.channelId));
        await defaultHandler(thread, message);
      },
    },
    resolveResourceId: ({ requestContext }) => `tenant:${requestContext.get('tenantId')}`,
  }
  ```

  `requestContext` is a required field on `ResolveResourceIdContext` and `ResolveThreadIdContext`. Code that builds these objects by hand, such as tests that call a resolver directly, now needs to pass one:

  ```ts
  // Before
  await resolveResourceId({ platform, thread, message, defaultResourceId });

  // After
  await resolveResourceId({ platform, thread, message, defaultResourceId, requestContext: new RequestContext() });
  ```

- Fixed workspace search and file listing ignoring `.gitignore` files below the workspace root. Content search and the file tree now apply every `.gitignore` relative to its own directory (for example, each repository in a multi-repo workspace) and skip `node_modules` unless the path points inside it. This keeps search from reading dependency files one by one on remote filesystems, and keeps file listings free of ignored and dependency files. (#27687)

  Filesystems that implement `walk` can now honor two new options: `includeGitignoreFiles` returns `.gitignore` files even when hidden entries are excluded, and `skipDirectoryNames` skips directories such as `node_modules` entirely.

  ```ts
  const entries = await filesystem.walk('.', {
    includeGitignoreFiles: true,
    skipDirectoryNames: ['node_modules'],
  });
  ```

- Add opt-in `bundler.pnpmWorkspaceSettings` to write pnpm install-policy settings to generated deployment workspaces. Supported keys are `allowBuilds`, `onlyBuiltDependencies`, `ignoredBuiltDependencies`, `minimumReleaseAge`, `minimumReleaseAgeExclude`, `trustPolicy`, `trustPolicyExclude`, `trustPolicyIgnoreAfter`, and `blockExoticSubdeps`; explicit values override copied source settings and any other key is rejected before pnpm runs. Existing behavior is unchanged when omitted. (#27708)

  ```ts
  import { Mastra } from '@mastra/core/mastra';

  export const mastra = new Mastra({
    bundler: {
      pnpmWorkspaceSettings: {
        blockExoticSubdeps: false,
      },
    },
  });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/deployer@1.79.0_

- Added iteration-aware final output processing so processors receive the current feedback-continuation iteration as its own assistant message and can transform each iteration independently. (#26346)

  Fixed feedback continuations with output processors so rewrites and redactions are preserved in `getFullOutput().text` and `steps[].text` instead of returning raw earlier-iteration text.

  Custom output processors that persist transcripts should use `messageList.cloneForPersistence()` to retain one assistant response with its original identity and order.

  ```ts
  // Before: may persist processor-visible response messages separately.
  const processor = {
    id: 'transcript-persister',
    processOutputResult: async ({ messages, messageList }) => {
      await saveMessages(messageList.get.response.db());
      return messages;
    },
  };

  // After: preserves one assistant response with its original identity and order.
  const processor = {
    id: 'transcript-persister',
    processOutputResult: async ({ messages, messageList }) => {
      await saveMessages(messageList.cloneForPersistence().get.response.db());
      return messages;
    },
  };
  ```

- Added thread rows with activity timestamps, trace counts, previews, root-agent model details, filtering and sorting. (#27652)

  **Breaking contract change:** Thread-row requests use `timeRange.traces` or `timeRange.threads` instead of `traces.timeRange`. Related-trace predicates are not supported in the new contract. Legacy identity requests remain supported for mixed package versions.

  ```ts
  // Before
  const before = { traces: { timeRange: { from, to } } };
  // After
  const after = { timeRange: { traces: { from, to } } };
  ```

- Added optional, experimental `getEventsInfo()` and `getEventsInspector()` members to `MCPServerBase`, plus the event info types (`MCPServerEventInfo`, `MCPServerEventStreamFrame`, `MCPServerEventsInspector`, …) and `MCP_SERVER_EVENT_ERROR_IDS`. Servers that declare MCP events use them to let Studio list, poll, stream and test-emit their event types. Servers without events don't need to implement them. (#27674)

  ```ts
  const info = server.getEventsInfo?.();
  console.log(info?.events.map(event => event.name));

  const inspector = server.getEventsInspector?.();
  const { events, cursor } = await inspector!.poll('ticket.created', { arguments: { project: 'web' } });
  ```

- Added an `embeddingDimension` option to Memory. When you know your embedder's output dimension, setting it lets semantic recall skip the probe embedding call it otherwise makes once per Memory instance. Startup then makes no billed embedding call and doesn't fail when the embedding provider is unreachable. The vector index name stays the same, so existing indexes keep working. If the configured value doesn't match the length of the embeddings the embedder returns, Memory throws a `MASTRA_MEMORY_EMBEDDING_DIMENSION_MISMATCH` error naming both values before creating or querying any vector index. (#27588)

  ```ts
  const memory = new Memory({
    embedder: 'openai/text-embedding-3-small',
    embeddingDimension: 1536,
    options: { semanticRecall: true },
  });
  ```

- Added `timeToFirstChunkMs` to trace and span queries. `queryTraces()` returns the root span's time to first chunk and `querySpans()` returns each span's, both `null` when no chunk was streamed. Both accept `timeToFirstChunkMs` filters with the same operators as `durationMs`. Stores that support it declare the `trace-query-time-to-first-chunk` feature. (#27277)

- Support embedding specification v4 models from AI SDK v7. Core accepts v4 embedding models and exports `embedV4`; Memory and RAG use the matching SDK to generate embeddings while preserving v1, v2, and v3 behavior. (#27710)

  _Also in: @mastra/memory@1.39.0, @mastra/rag@2.7.0_

- Added a `datasetIds` filter to dataset listing, so you can fetch specific datasets by ID in one request. All matches come back on one page, up to 100 IDs. (#27255)

  ```ts
  const { datasets } = await client.listDatasets({ datasetIds: ['dataset-1', 'dataset-2'] });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/client-js@1.56.0, @mastra/libsql@1.27.0, @mastra/mongodb@1.24.0, @mastra/mysql@0.14.0, @mastra/pg@1.32.0, @mastra/server@1.79.0, @mastra/spanner@1.10.0_

- Added the root span's `name` and `spanType` as trace-scope fields for trace queries and trace aggregates. (#27321)

  - `parseTraceQueryRequest()` and `parseTraceAggregateRequest()` accept `name` and `spanType` in trace-scope `where` predicates.
  - `planTraceAggregate()` accepts them in `groupBy` and as `countDistinct.name` / `countDistinct.spanType`.
  - Trace-scope field discovery lists both with value suggestions, and the `aggregate.dimensions` list includes them.

  Inside `spans.some` and `spans.none`, `name` and `spanType` still match any span in the trace.

  ```ts
  const plan = planTraceAggregate(
    parseTraceAggregateRequest({
      timeRange: { from: '2026-08-01T00:00:00Z', to: '2026-08-08T00:00:00Z' },
      where: { op: 'eq', left: { path: 'spanType' }, right: { literal: 'workflow_run' } },
      groupBy: ['name'],
      measures: ['count', 'errorRate'],
    }),
  );
  ```

  Stores that support these fields declare the new `trace-query-root-span-fields` storage feature.

- Agent schedules can now send an idle wake to the worker that has claimed the thread. Set `ifIdle.requireClaimedOwner: true` on the schedule, or return it from the `prepare` hook. This works the same way as `agent.sendSignal`: the claim holder runs the turn with its own claim stream options. If no owner answers, the fire fails with `outcome: 'failed'`. Before this change, the option was dropped, so whichever worker took the fire ran the turn itself. (#27896)

  ```ts
  await mastra.schedules.create({
    agentId: 'support',
    cron: '0 * * * *',
    prompt: 'Check in',
    threadId,
    resourceId,
    ifIdle: { behavior: 'wake', requireClaimedOwner: true },
  });
  ```

- Added `querySpans()` filters and response fields for span context and parent/root entity information. For example: (#27272)

  ```ts
  where: { op: 'eq', left: { path: 'threadId' }, right: { literal: 'thread-123' } }
  ```

  **Breaking:** `querySpans()` no longer accepts `model` and `provider` filters or returns `cost`, `inputTruncated`, `outputTruncated` and `errorTruncated`. Model/provider values, name matching, duration filtering and previews remain available.

  The new filters require an updated storage adapter.

- Trace and span queries can now sort by `timeToFirstChunkMs`, in either direction. Missing values sort last, and pages stay stable when values tie. Stores that support it declare the `trace-query-time-to-first-chunk-sort` feature. (#27473)

  ```typescript
  const page = await storage.queryTraces(
    planTraceQuery(
      parseTraceQueryRequest({ timeRange, orderBy: [{ field: 'timeToFirstChunkMs', direction: 'desc' }] }),
    ),
  );
  ```

- Span records now carry `timeToFirstChunkMs`, the milliseconds from span start to the first streamed chunk. The storage exporters fill it in, so storage adapters save the value without computing it. (#27140)

- Added `completionStartTime` to agent run and model step span attributes, a `getTimeToFirstChunkMs()` helper that derives time to first chunk for any span, and carried the first-chunk clock through durable and evented agent runs so their generation and agent run spans report it too. (#26998)

- Added `backgroundTasks.untilIdleMaxIdleMs` to set the default idle timeout for `stream({ untilIdle: true })`. Before, calls that did not pass `maxIdleMs` always closed after 5 minutes without new events. This included calls Mastra makes internally, such as waking an agent after `sendMessage`. A background task that ran longer than that finished without a continuation turn. A per-call `untilIdle: { maxIdleMs }` still takes precedence. (#27757)

  ```ts
  const mastra = new Mastra({
    backgroundTasks: {
      enabled: true,
      untilIdleMaxIdleMs: 30 * 60_000,
    },
  });
  ```

- Added an opt-in `apply_patch` workspace tool that creates, updates, moves and deletes several files in one call. The tool checks the whole patch before it writes anything. If a write fails partway, it tries to undo the changes it already made. The tool is off by default. Enable it per tool: (#27913)

  ```ts
  new Workspace({
    filesystem: new LocalFilesystem({ basePath: './workspace' }),
    tools: { [WORKSPACE_TOOLS.FILESYSTEM.APPLY_PATCH]: { enabled: true } },
  });
  ```

  Setting the top-level `tools.enabled` does not turn it on, so existing agents keep using `write_file` and `edit_file`. Paths follow the same containment, read-before-write and write-lock rules as the other filesystem tools.

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed agent runs that were aborted while a tool was running finishing with `finishReason: 'tool-calls'`. When a tool stops because the run's abort signal fired, the run now ends with `finishReason: 'aborted'`, emits an `abort` chunk, and calls `onAbort`, the same as an abort during the model response. (#27748)

- Fixed agent runs that kept calling the model and tools after losing their thread lease. When lease renewal fails, the run is now aborted instead of running beside the run that took over the thread on another process. Applies to regular, durable and evented agents. (#27581)

- The error for a model with an unrecognized `specificationVersion` now lists `v4` (AI SDK v7) among the supported versions. (#27516)

- Fixed session messages starting while another agent runs on the same thread. When an `AgentController` uses an application's agent and has no `pubsub` of its own, its agents without their own `pubsub` now share the application's event bus. Session messages now wait for the other run to finish. A `pubsub` passed to the controller still takes precedence. (#27631)

- Fixed two cases where an AgentController tool approval could get out of sync with its stored run: (#27371)

  - **A failed resume no longer loses the answer.** If approving or declining a tool call fails while the run is still waiting on it, the approval prompt is raised again (`tool_approval_required`) so the answer can be retried. Previously a retry returned `{ accepted: false, reason: 'not_pending' }` and the run stayed suspended.
  - **Detaching no longer declines a pending approval.** `session.thread.detachFromCurrent()` now leaves a parked tool approval pending in storage, as documented, instead of declining it. It can be answered later, for example with `respondToPersistedToolApproval`.

- Update provider registry and model documentation with latest models and providers (`9b60f12`)

- Fixed agent turns stopping early when an awaited background tool returns no result. The agent now sends `null` to the model and continues. (#27482)

- Fixed `untilIdle` continuations losing a completed background task's result when newer messages push it outside the configured history window. (#27205)

- Fixed `workspace_grep` hanging for minutes on large workspaces. A directory search that runs on the host now has a 60-second budget. The budget covers filesystems without native grep, including the default `LocalFilesystem`, as well as the fallback used when a filesystem's native grep fails. Once the budget runs out or the agent run is aborted, the search stops starting new reads (reads already in progress finish) and returns the matches found so far. The summary line marks these results as incomplete and says how many files were scanned. The fallback log message now says why native grep failed (for example, a timeout) instead of always reporting an unsupported pattern. (#27464)

- Reduced durable agent snapshot size: tool results are no longer stored twice per step, so long-running durable agents with large tool outputs write much smaller snapshots. `stopWhen` conditions still receive the full step content. (#27634)

- Added `bufferSize` option to `SystemPromptScrubber` and `ModerationProcessor`. Set `bufferSize: 200` to check per sentence instead of per chunk (fewer LLM calls, catches content split across chunks). Default unchanged; will become the default in a future major. (#27510)

  ```ts
  new SystemPromptScrubber({ model, bufferSize: 200 });
  new ModerationProcessor({ model, bufferSize: 200 });
  ```

- Fixed live scorers passed at call time (`agent.stream(..., { scorers })`) or through `defaultOptions.scorers` never running unless the same scorer was also registered on the agent or on Mastra. Such scorers now run and save their scores on every agent engine, including durable and evented agents. (#27714)

- Added `byUser` to the `approved` channel tool display event, matching `denied`. A custom `toolDisplay` function can now show who approved a tool call (for example "Approved by Alice"), not only who denied it. The default approved card also names the approving user. `byUser` is undefined in DMs. (#27584)

  ```ts
  toolDisplay: event => {
    if (event.kind === 'approved') {
      return { kind: 'post', message: `Approved by ${event.byUser ?? 'you'}` };
    }
  };
  ```

- Channel streaming: text the agent writes before its first tool call now posts as its own message in `toolDisplay: 'grouped'` mode. An opening acknowledgement lands above the Thinking Steps widget instead of under it. (#27350)

  The plan widget in `'grouped'` and `'timeline'` modes is quieter. The header is a fixed "Working…" label and no longer echoes the current tool. Each task row shows the tool's `description` argument (first line only) when it has one, and falls back to `toolName firstArg` otherwise.

  Grouped mode is an adapter option on the channel provider:

  ```ts
  import { Mastra } from '@mastra/core';
  import { SlackProvider } from '@mastra/slack';

  export const mastra = new Mastra({
    channels: {
      slack: new SlackProvider({
        refreshToken: process.env.SLACK_APP_CONFIG_REFRESH_TOKEN,
        toolDisplay: 'grouped',
      }),
    },
  });
  ```

- Fixed an agent run that had lost its thread lease running tools next to the run that took over the thread. Lease renewals run every third of the lease TTL, so a run whose lease was taken over could keep calling tools until its next renewal. A run that holds a thread lease now checks the lease owner before each tool call. If another run owns the lease, the run is aborted and the tool does not run. If the lease backend can't answer, the local lease deadline decides as before. (#27749)

- Classifiers now accept `refusal` answers from decision models instead of throwing. Previously, when a decision model refused one question, `decide()` failed with `Answer '<question>' does not match its question type.` and any workflow `.classifier()` step failed the run. (#27488)

  A refused question now returns `{ type: 'refusal' }` while the other questions keep their answers. `RefusalAnswer` is exported and included in every `ClassifierAnswer` type. Refusals have no `choice`, `score`, or `probability`, so equality checks such as `answers.route.choice === 'billing'` evaluate to `false`; check `type === 'refusal'` before relying on numeric fields.

  ```ts
  const { answers } = await classifier.decide({ state });

  if (answers.unsafe.type === 'refusal') {
    // Route to review, block, or apply a default.
  }
  ```

  Classifier scorers fail with an error naming the refused question, and `ModelSelectionProcessor` keeps the configured model and reports `abstained: 'refused'`.

- Classifier evaluation traces now record the classifier as their entity, so they show the classifier name on the Traces page instead of "(not set)" and can be filtered and grouped like agents, workflows and scorers. `EntityType` gains a `classifier` value. (#27756)

  _Also in: @mastra/client-js@1.56.0_

- Fixed `Classifier.decide()` crashing with `state.map is not a function` on decision providers built for `@ai-sdk/provider` 4.0.25 and newer, such as `@ai-sdk/typesafe-ai` 3.0.16+. String and JSON state are now converted to `text` and `json` parts before the provider is called, matching the AI SDK. Arrays are passed through only when every element is already a state part; other JSON arrays are wrapped in a `json` part. (#27298)

- Fixed `autoResumeSuspendedTools` leaving a resumed plain tool marked as suspended. This happened when the model resumed a regular (non-agent, non-workflow) tool under a new tool call id. The original suspension was never cleared. Every later turn asked the model to resume the tool again. Reloaded clients also showed the call as still pending. The original suspension is now cleared, and a `tool-call-resumed` chunk is emitted for it. This applies to the standard, evented and durable agents. (#27784)

- Fixed approval after a host disconnect without cancelling the parked agent run. (#27907)

- Fixed workspace search and file listing so a deeper `.gitignore` can re-include what a parent ignores (for example `!keep.log` in a subdirectory overriding `*.log`), matching git. Search results from a filesystem's native `grep` now also apply `.gitignore` files in subdirectories of the search path. (#27695)

- Classifier evaluation spans now show what the classifier was asked and what it decided. The span input holds the `state` and the questions, and the span output holds the answers, so routing decisions are visible in traces. Set `hideInput` and `hideOutput` in `tracingOptions` to keep them out of exported traces. (#27243)

- Fixed durable and evented agents losing the conversation when a client tool is approved or declined on an agent without memory. The finish chunk now carries the same `messages` history as the default agent, so the follow-up model request keeps the original user prompt, the tool call and the tool result. The client tool also runs once: the first stream of a durable run stays open after suspension, and it no longer re-runs a tool the approve or decline request can execute. A server-side approve, which sends no client tools, still runs the tool on that open stream. (#27874)

  _Also in: @mastra/client-js@1.56.0_

- Fixed durable agents not forwarding a declined sub-agent tool approval to the sub-agent. When a supervisor declines a tool approval raised by a sub-agent, the decline and its reason now reach the suspended sub-agent run, matching the approve path and the regular `Agent`. Declining the supervisor's own tool approval still denies the call locally. (#27374)

- Fixed durable and evented agents placing `isTaskComplete` feedback after the model's next tool call and its result. The feedback now comes right before the model's retry, as it does with regular agents, so the model reads it as the reason for continuing. Fixes #27548. (#27743)

- Fixed durable and evented agents ignoring per-model `modelSettings` in a model list. When an agent is configured with `model: [{ model, modelSettings: { temperature: 0.1 } }, ...]`, each entry's settings now reach the provider on every attempt, including retries and fallbacks, instead of falling back to call-time settings. (#27727)

- Fixed DurableAgent ignoring the reserved `mastra__threadId` and `mastra__resourceId` request-context keys. Durable and evented agents now route memory to the thread and resource set on the request context, as the regular Agent does, instead of to the thread or resource named in the call's `memory` options. (#27575)

- Fixed durable agent resumes that lose a resume race or arrive after the run finished (#27365). (#27379)

  - A second approval of the same tool call fails right away with `AGENT_RESUME_ALREADY_CLAIMED` while the first resume runs in the same process, including when both approvals arrive at the same moment. Before, it waited for the whole resumed turn or about 5 s and then failed with `DURABLE_AGENT_EXECUTION_CONFLICT`.
  - A resume that loses the race reports the error only on its own stream. Clients following the run no longer get an error event or a span ended with an error while the winning resume completes.
  - Resuming a run whose snapshot was already deleted (for example because another instance finished it) now fails with `AGENT_RESUME_NO_SNAPSHOT_FOUND` and no longer writes a new `pending` workflow row.
  - A resume that loses the race no longer blocks the winning run's memory writes.
  - The durable execution lease now follows `MASTRA_AGENT_THREAD_LEASE_TTL_MS` (renewed every third of it). The default TTL drops from 30 s to 15 s, so a run left by a crashed process becomes recoverable sooner.

- You can now resume durable agents (`createDurableAgent`, `createInngestAgent`) with the same `resumeGenerate(resumeData, { runId })` call you use with `Agent`. (#27299)

  ```ts
  // Before (durable-only order, still supported but deprecated)
  await durableAgent.resumeGenerate(runId, { approved: true });

  // After (same as Agent)
  await durableAgent.resumeGenerate({ approved: true }, { runId });
  ```

  One deprecated call shape now throws instead of guessing: a string run ID followed by resume data that itself contains a string `runId`, with no third argument, for example `resumeGenerate('run-A', { runId: 'x', approved: true })`. To keep using the deprecated order, pass an explicit options argument: `resumeGenerate('run-A', { runId: 'x', approved: true }, {})`.

  _Also in: @mastra/inngest@1.10.7_

- Fixed durable and evented agents dropping earlier tool calls from the prompt on a second output processor retry. When a processor called `abort(reason, { retry: true })` more than once in a run that had already made tool calls, the next request lost the earlier tool call and its result, which broke the provider prompt cache. Durable and evented agents now keep the full history across retries, matching the standard agent loop (#27561). (#27758)

- Fixed a durable `AgentController` session hanging after a prompt left unanswered for longer than `MASTRA_SUSPENDED_RUN_TTL_MS`. Later messages now run and finish as usual. The dropped prompt can still be answered. (#27632)

- Fixed `stopWhen` on durable agents (`DurableAgent`, evented and Inngest) receiving steps without `content`. Predicates such as `steps.at(-1)?.content.some(...)` used to throw and end the run with a stream error. They now see the same step content that `stream.steps` reports. (#27524)

- Fixed `resumeGenerate()` and `getFullOutput()` on durable agents sometimes never completing when a resumed run suspends again. (#27384)

- Fixed durable and evented agents reporting no token usage for a step that suspends on a tool call. `generate()` results and `tool-call-approval` / `tool-call-suspended` chunks now include that step's usage, matching the regular agent. (#27493)

- Fixed durable and evented agents ignoring `generateTitle.emitEvent` and `onTitleGenerated`. Streams from these agents now include the transient `data-thread-title` chunk before `finish`, and `memory.onTitleGenerated` is called with the saved title, matching the standard agent. (#27606)

- Fixed durable and evented agents returning an empty `response.messages` when a `processOutputResult` processor rejects the final answer. `response.messages` now contains the assistant's draft, matching `text`, `steps` and the normal loop, so apps can still show the rejected reply to a reviewer. (#27737)

- Fixed evented workflow restarts replaying completed steps and ensured recovered durable-agent streams finish without repeating saved effects. (#27852)

- Fixed `run.stream()` on the evented workflow engine reporting `workflow-finish` with `workflowStatus: 'success'` when the run failed. The evented engine now emits the failed step's `workflow-step-result` before finishing, matching the default engine, so stream consumers and UIs see `failed`. (#27559)

- Fixed evented `.foreach()` runs where more than one iteration fails. The run now keeps the first iteration's error instead of the last one, and fails exactly once. Previously, an iteration failing after the run had already failed replaced the stored error and published a second `workflow.fail`. Time-travel replays that hit a failure again also failed twice. (#26651)

- Server model usage telemetry now counts tokens as model calls finish and reports them every 15 minutes and on shutdown as a new `mastra_model_token_usage_v2` event. Each `mastra_model_token_usage_v2` event contains only tokens the reporting process used, so redeploys, replicas, and lost home directories can't make it resend earlier usage. The startup `mastra_model_token_usage` event is still sent, unchanged, for compatibility, and can still resend earlier usage in those cases. Set `MASTRA_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` to turn off telemetry. (#26986)

- Fixed durable agent startup recovery so unrelated runs can recover concurrently. Timed-out attempts are checked again after settlement without starting duplicate executions or releasing live ownership. (#27768)

- xAI models now accept `xhigh` reasoning effort. Groq, xAI, Together AI, DeepInfra, Cerebras and Alibaba models now run on the AI SDK v7 provider packages, like DeepSeek. (#26530)

  ```ts
  await agent.generate('Prove that there are infinitely many primes.', {
    providerOptions: { xai: { reasoningEffort: 'xhigh' } },
  });
  ```

- Fixed `fetchWithRetry` leaving the bodies of failed HTTP responses open when it retried or gave up, which could keep connections busy until garbage collection. Failed responses are now cancelled before retrying or throwing; successful responses are still returned unread. (#27439)

- `Agent.reorderModels()` and `Agent.updateModelInModelList()` now return `true` when the edit was applied and `false` when it was not (no model list, unknown id, or a function-valued `model`). Function-valued models are left untouched so per-request model selection keeps working. (#27192)

  ```ts
  if (!agent.updateModelInModelList({ id, enabled: false })) {
    // edit was not applied
  }
  ```

- Fixed output stream processors dropping text they were still holding when a model stream ended. A reply shorter than one `BatchPartsProcessor` batch (for example with `emitOnNonText: false`) used to stream and save as empty; held parts are now released at the end of each model stream and passed through the processors that come after the one that held them. (#27565)

- Fixed a Session that follows a thread keeping a parked tool call after another Session aborted the run, or after the resumed run ended with a non-success finish such as `content-filter`. The follower now ends the run and retracts the call with `tool_suspension_cancelled`, so `suspensions.hasPending()` returns false. This also works when the follower runs in a different Mastra instance that shares the same pubsub (#26997). (#27786)

- Fixed a Session that follows a thread keeping a tool call marked as pending after the run was resumed on another instance and completed. The follower now drops the answered call, so `suspensions.hasPending()` returns false and aborting no longer emits a spurious `tool_suspension_cancelled` event. (#27226)

- Fixed durable, evented, and Inngest agent streams to reject when `stopWhen` throws, matching plain agents. (#26921)

  Previously, these stream consumers could handle this failure as an `error` chunk. Catch the `fullStream` rejection instead:

  ```ts
  try {
    for await (const chunk of output.fullStream) {
      // Handle streamed chunks.
    }
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle the error thrown by stopWhen.
  }
  ```

  Durable transports reconstruct the error from its serialized name and message. The original stack, cause, and subclass identity do not survive a cross-process boundary. Core durable and evented workflows classify this failure with the reserved internal `DURABLE_AGENT_STOP_WHEN_FAILED` identifier. Inngest's serve transport uses the memoized step's `stop-when-` id prefix because its `StepError` serialization does not retain that identifier or its details. Any failure inside that Inngest step is therefore classified as a `stopWhen` failure.

  _Also in: @mastra/inngest@1.10.7_

- Fixed Gemini 3 agents with working memory failing with "Function call is missing a thought_signature" when continuing a turn (for example after a client tool result). Gemini signs only the first tool call in a parallel step; when that call was `updateWorkingMemory`, removing it from saved history left the step without a signed first call. The next tool call in the step is now saved with Google's documented `skip_thought_signature_validator` signature, so the step replays on any Gemini client. (#27787)

  _Also in: @mastra/memory@1.39.0_

- Fixed the goal judge reading the `TrailingAssistantGuard`'s synthetic `Continue.` turn as the latest user message. On models that reject a trailing assistant turn (Claude 4.6+, Gemini 3+), the judge was told the user had said `Continue.` when they had not, hiding the real latest user message and miscounting assistant steps since it. The guard now marks its continuation, and the goal judge skips it. (#27503)

- Simplified Azure gateway defaults and synchronous stream handling without changing runtime behavior. (#27646)

- Fixed replies being saved as a separate assistant message on Gemini 3+ (and Anthropic 4.6+ with structured output) when the input ends on an assistant message. The reply is now saved into that assistant message, the same as with other providers. The synthetic user turn ("Continue." or "Generate the structured response.") Mastra sends to these providers is also no longer kept in the run's message list after the request is built. (#27503)

- Fixed output stream processors that hold parts (such as `BatchPartsProcessor` and `PIIDetector`) re-emitting or leaking text when a model call failed. When a call is retried, the dropped attempt's text is no longer repeated before the retry's reply. When a call errors or is aborted, the text it was holding is dropped instead of being sent, and the error or abort still reaches the caller. Parts a processor holds back and sends again now reach the stream instead of getting stuck, so the reply's `text-end` is no longer lost. (#27615)

- Fixed live DurableAgent observers attaching to runs owned by a different agent ID while preserving same-ID observation. Prepared runs now retain the DurableAgent's effective ID and name so ownership remains consistent after a restart. (#27353)

- Stop an agent run as soon as another replica asks to abort it, even after the run has lost its thread lease. Previously the abort request was ignored once the lease was gone, so the run kept calling tools until its own lease renewal noticed the loss. (#27738)

- Fixed token usage being counted twice after a tool call suspends and the run resumes. Spans and metrics for the resumed run now report only the tokens used after resuming, including when the resumed run suspends again. (#27634)

- Fixed automatic durable agent recovery stopping after temporary storage or pubsub errors. Skipped runs are retried when the backend becomes available, without another restart or manual recovery. (#27354)

- Fixed slow provider listing in `mastra dev`. Opening the model picker in Studio (`GET /api/agents/providers`) no longer re-reads the shared provider cache from disk for every provider, so the request drops from seconds to well under a second. (#26711)

- Failed turns on the default Agent now save the same history as durable agents. (#27571)

  - A reply that fails mid-stream keeps the text streamed so far, followed by the error, instead of only the error.
  - When an input processor throws, the user message and the error are saved to memory, and the stream ends with an `error` chunk instead of the run failing with nothing saved.

- Fixed live scorers passed to `stream()`/`generate()` via `scorers`, or set in an agent's `defaultOptions.scorers`, being skipped on the Inngest engine when they are not registered on the agent or on Mastra. They now run after the call, as they already do on the default and evented engines. (#27719)

  _Also in: @mastra/inngest@1.10.7_

- Fixed tool calls disappearing from conversation history when a message or reminder arrived while the tool was still running. The call and its result now stay in history, so the model sees its own completed work on the next turn. (#26963)

- Fixed a thread that stopped starting runs after a run waiting on a tool prompt was followed by another run on the same thread. Once that other run finished, every new message, signal or notification was reported delivered but never ran until the process restarted. The waiting run now gives the thread up when another run takes it over, and it can still be resumed with the user's answer. A run that is still waiting on its prompt keeps holding the thread as before. (#27545)

- Fixed `LocalSandbox` commands that could hang past their timeout. When a command times out, it is asked to stop and then force-killed two seconds later if it is still running, including child processes that keep its output open. Previously a command that ignored the stop request kept the tool call waiting forever. (#26879)

- Stop an agent run whose thread lease can no longer be confirmed. Previously, if lease renewals failed with an error or never answered (for example during a Redis outage on one process), the run kept calling the model and tools after its lease expired, alongside the successor run that took over the thread. Each run now aborts once one lease TTL has passed since its last confirmed acquire or renewal, the same way it already aborts when a renewal reports the lease lost. (#27731)

- Fixed assistant message merging slowing down quadratically with the number of message parts. A step with thousands of reasoning items (common with OpenAI Responses reasoning) could previously block the event loop for tens of seconds; merging is now linear and produces the same merged messages. (#27912)

- Fixed evented `foreach` restarts hanging when the next iteration had not started and nested workflow iterations re-running already completed child steps. (#26368)

- Fixed MCP tool results containing images or audio being dropped by AI SDK v5 models and rejected by AI SDK v7 models such as Amazon Bedrock (`unsupported tool content part type: image-data`). MCP media content is now passed to every model in the format it supports. (#26662)

- Fixed a mode's `availableTools` being dropped after the run paused for the user. Approving or declining a tool call, answering `ask_user` or `request_access`, or rejecting a plan used to continue the run with every tool the agent has, including tools the mode excludes. The continuation now gets the same restricted tool set as the first model call. (#27864)

- Fixed notification dispatch stalling behind notifications it leaves pending. When a delivery policy holds notifications for another process's dispatcher (or they wait on an active run), `dispatchDueNotifications` now reads past them instead of re-reading the same first page every pass, so notifications behind them are still delivered. `limit` now counts only the notifications a pass delivers or fails. (#27426)

- Fixed notifications that ran out of delivery attempts disappearing from the inbox. The `notification-inbox` tool's default `list` now includes `failed` notifications, reporting them as `failed` with their last delivery error, and marks them seen so they show up once rather than on every list. (#27453)

- Fixed `observe()` hanging forever on a durable or evented agent run that was aborted while waiting for tool approval. Once the rejected approval or resume releases the run, observers now receive the `abort` and `finish` chunks and close. (#27923)

- Fixed agent runs stopping when background observational memory buffering fails. The failure is still reported as an `om_buffering_failed` event, and the turn keeps going. A failed synchronous observation still follows the configured failure policy. (#27438)

- Fixed a prompt waiting on the user disappearing from the session's display when another run on the same thread finished. The prompt could still be answered, but nothing showed it. Only the ending run's own prompts are now cleared. (#27555)

- Fixed `get_process_output` with `wait: true` hanging after a background command had exited. When the command left a child running that still held its output open (`cmd &`, or a child of `timeout`), the wait never returned. It now returns once the command exits and its output has had a moment to drain, and says when a process the command started is still running. That process can still be stopped with `kill_process`, and is still stopped with the sandbox. A foreground command that starts a process in the background (`cmd &`) now returns once its shell exits rather than at its timeout, and the process it left behind is stopped when it returns. Use `background: true` for processes that should keep running. (#27578)

- Fixed notifications that failed on their first delivery attempt. Delivery now retries automatically, up to five attempts, instead of staying pending forever. Retries preserve the claimed-owner requirement. (#25837)

- Fixed `Agent.stream()` ending with a `finish` chunk (reason `stop`) when an output processor aborts in `processOutputResult`. The stream now ends with a `tripwire` chunk and `status` is `'tripwire'`, matching durable agents and other processor tripwires. (#27377)

- Fixed resumed agent spans to remain children of the initial agent run across repeated suspensions, matching durable agent tracing. (#27149)

- `respondToPersistedToolApproval` now accepts `alwaysAllowCategory`. Approving a tool call restored after a restart with "always allow" grants the tool's category to the run's thread, matching a live approval. (#27222)

  ```ts
  await session.respondToPersistedToolApproval({ toolCallId, approved: true, alwaysAllowCategory: true });
  ```

- Fixed trajectory scorers in `runEvals` and dataset experiments getting a flat list of tool calls instead of the full trace when storage is configured. `runEvals` also no longer waits up to 6 seconds per item before scoring. (#27271)

- Fixed a TypeScript error (TS2345) when chaining processor steps with `.then()` or `.parallel()`. Steps built with `createStep(processor)` now accept the output of a previous processor step, so the output guardrails workflow from the docs type-checks: (#27764)

  ```ts
  const workflow = createWorkflow({
    id: 'output-guardrails',
    inputSchema: ProcessorStepSchema,
    outputSchema: ProcessorStepSchema,
  })
    .then(createStep(new TokenLimiterProcessor({ limit: 1000 })))
    .then(createStep(new BatchPartsProcessor()))
    .commit();
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/inngest@1.10.7_

- Fixed approved tools added by an input processor running with the suspended run's request context. When you approve a tool call with `approveToolCall({ requestContext })`, a tool that an input processor added now sees the request context you passed, just like a tool defined on the agent. (#27570)

- Fixed experiment comparisons when item, scorer, or experiment IDs match built-in object property names such as `constructor`, `toString`, or `__proto__`. Comparisons previously threw or dropped those entries; they now group and report them like any other ID. (#27718)

- Fixed a misleading "No memory is configured but resourceId and threadId were passed in args" warning logged every time Observational Memory ran its Observer or Reflector. The warning now fires only when `memory.thread` and `memory.resource` are passed to an agent that has no memory, not when the ids come from the request context (auth middleware, AgentController), which every agent in the request inherits. (#27865)

- Fixed several ways a suspended agent run could be resumed twice, never, or after it was aborted. (#27791)

  - **Approving after abort:** approving, declining, or resuming a run after `abortRunStream()` / `abortThreadStream()` hit its suspension now fails with `AGENT_RESUME_ABORTED_RUN` instead of running the tool or starting another model request. Fixes #27617.
  - **`listSuspendedRuns()` after resume:** `DurableAgent` now marks a resumed run as running as soon as it resumes, so it drops out of `listSuspendedRuns()` right away, including in other processes. A second resume of the same run fails with `AGENT_RESUME_ALREADY_CLAIMED` instead of running twice. Fixes #27619.
  - **Resumer process dies:** a running resume keeps renewing its claim. If the process dies, a later resume or approval fails with `AGENT_RESUME_ALREADY_CLAIMED` until the claim lapses, then takes the run over (or fails with `AGENT_RESUME_RUN_FINISHED` when recovery already completed it). Fixes #27366.

  _Also in: @mastra/inngest@1.10.7_

- Fixed durable agent runs saving every tool output a second time in their final snapshot, which made the last snapshot write noticeably larger than the earlier ones. (#27672)

- Fix experiment comparisons to average repeated trial scores per item instead of keeping only the last score. Items retain equal weight when experiments have different numbers of trials. (#27705)

- Fixed notifications getting stuck when the delivery policy throws while the notification is being sent. The notification now records a failed delivery attempt and is retried by the notification dispatcher, up to the attempt limit, instead of staying pending forever. (#27736)

- Fixed the 30-second default timeout for `LocalSandbox` foreground commands. A command run without a timeout could previously wait forever. Configured defaults and per-command timeouts are honored, including `0` to disable the timeout, and cloned sandboxes keep the configured default. Background processes keep their existing lifetime. (#26878)

- Fixed `respondToToolSuspension()` hanging when answering a tool call (such as `ask_user`) that was parked during a run you aborted. The resumed run now streams to the Session and completes normally, instead of being silently dropped. (#26899)

- Fixed system messages added by input processors (such as the `TaskSignalProvider` task-list instruction) going missing when an `Agent` resumes after tool approval. Fixes #24844. (#27372)

  Resumed runs now reuse the system prompt from the suspended run, matching durable agents. Instructions or system messages passed only at resume time no longer replace it.

- Fixed `Agent.resumeGenerate()` and `Agent.resumeStream()` dropping `returnScorerData` on a plain Agent. A run started with `returnScorerData: true` now returns scoring data after a tool-approval or suspend resume, including when the resume happens in a different process or `defaultOptions` disable it. Passing `returnScorerData` to the resume call still overrides the run's value. (#27747)

- Fixed lost messages when a per-step save fails. With `savePerStep` enabled, a failed write used to drop the drained messages from the unsaved set, so no later save wrote them and the user's message or the step's output disappeared from the thread. Failed saves now mark those messages unsaved again, and the next save writes them. (#27556)

- Clarified that schedule `onError` and `onAbort` hooks only observe failures and aborts the schedule worker sees directly. Threaded schedules report failures after a run starts on the thread's own run, not through these hooks. (#27812)

- Scorer run and scorer step spans now record the scorer name as `entityName`, falling back to the scorer ID. Previously scorer traces had no entity name, so filtering or grouping traces by entity name left them out. (#27684)

- Fixed authenticated agent controller sessions failing with `AGENT_MEMORY_THREAD_RESOURCE_MISMATCH` when auth uses `mapUserToResourceId`. The controller now sets the reserved memory resource and thread keys to the session's own resource and thread. Other auth values in the request context are kept. (#27304)

- Fixed channel conversations (Slack, Discord, Telegram and others) never getting an automatic title. New channel threads now start without a title, like any other thread, so `generateTitle` names them from the conversation. (#27251)

  Before, every new channel thread was saved with a generic label such as `slack conversation`, which counted as an existing title and blocked title generation. With `generateTitle` turned off, new channel threads now appear untitled in Studio, which lists them by creation date, instead of showing that label. Existing threads keep their current title.

- Fixed trace query validation errors for an oversized `in` / `notIn` set pointing at the whole `where` predicate instead of its `set`. Zod 4.6.7 stopped reporting which union branch failed in this case. (#27699)

- Bump for npm (#27221)

  _Also in: @mastra/acp@0.4.3, @mastra/agent-browser@0.5.4, @mastra/agent-builder@1.1.30, @mastra/agentcore@0.5.2, @mastra/agentfs@0.2.2, @mastra/ai-sdk@1.11.2, @mastra/apple-container@0.5.1, @mastra/archil@0.3.1, @mastra/arize@1.3.26, @mastra/arthur@0.4.26, @mastra/astra@1.1.2, @mastra/auth@1.1.4, @mastra/auth-auth0@1.2.5, @mastra/auth-better-auth@1.1.8, @mastra/auth-clerk@1.3.1, @mastra/auth-cloud@1.2.7, @mastra/auth-firebase@1.1.4, @mastra/auth-google@0.1.6, @mastra/auth-neon@0.3.4, @mastra/auth-okta@0.2.5, @mastra/auth-studio@1.3.10, @mastra/auth-supabase@1.1.5, @mastra/auth-workos@1.6.7, @mastra/azure@0.3.2, @mastra/azure-ai-search@0.1.1, @mastra/blaxel@0.10.1, @mastra/braintrust@1.3.23, @mastra/brightdata@0.3.2, @mastra/browser-firecrawl@0.2.4, @mastra/browser-viewer@0.2.5, @mastra/chroma@1.1.4, @mastra/claude@0.3.3, @mastra/clickhouse@1.26.0, @mastra/client-js@1.56.0, @mastra/cloudflare@1.7.5, @mastra/cloudflare-d1@1.4.4, @mastra/cloudflare-sandbox@0.5.3, @mastra/code-sdk@1.15.0, @mastra/codemod@1.1.7, @mastra/connect@1.1.0, @mastra/convex@1.7.5, @mastra/couchbase@1.1.3, @mastra/cursor@0.3.3, @mastra/datadog@1.4.18, @mastra/daytona@0.11.3, @mastra/deepeval@0.1.20, @mastra/deployer@1.79.0, @mastra/deployer-cloud@1.79.0, @mastra/deployer-cloudflare@1.2.38, @mastra/deployer-netlify@1.2.38, @mastra/deployer-sandbox@0.3.23, @mastra/deployer-vercel@1.2.38, @mastra/discord@1.3.1, @mastra/docker@0.12.0, @mastra/dsql@1.5.4, @mastra/duckdb@1.15.0, @mastra/dynamodb@1.4.4, @mastra/e2b@0.15.0, @mastra/e2b-desktop@0.1.9, @mastra/editor@0.16.4, @mastra/elasticsearch@1.4.5, @mastra/elysia@0.1.19, @mastra/evals@1.10.8, @mastra/express@1.5.23, @mastra/factory@0.24.0, @mastra/fastembed@1.3.4, @mastra/fastify@1.5.23, @mastra/files-sdk@0.3.2, @mastra/gcs@0.3.4, @mastra/github-signals@0.5.2, @mastra/google-cloud-pubsub@1.1.4, @mastra/google-drive@0.2.3, @mastra/hono@1.7.21, @mastra/inngest@1.10.7, @mastra/isolated-vm@0.1.3, @mastra/koa@1.7.23, @mastra/laminar@1.3.28, @mastra/lance