voice-speechify@0.14.2, @mastra/voice-xai-realtime@0.2.16, @mastra/voyageai@0.4.2, @mastra/weaviate@0.1.3_

- Fixed `System message must be at the beginning` errors from models served through Chat Completions APIs (e.g. Qwen3.5/3.6 or Gemma 3 on vLLM). Multiple leading system messages are now merged into one for Chat Completions providers. Prompts are left unchanged when an earlier system message carries its own provider options (such as a cache breakpoint). Providers with a dedicated system field (Anthropic, Google, Bedrock) and the OpenAI Responses API are unchanged. (#27307)

- Fixed skill tools (`skill`, `skill_read`, `skill_search`) pausing for approval when `requireToolApproval` is enabled globally. Previously every skill load in an approval-enabled agent (including durable agents) suspended for an approval nobody needed. (#27296)

- Fixed stored skills ignoring the `metadata` filter. `skills.list({ metadata })` and `skills.listResolved({ metadata })` now return only skills whose resolved version metadata matches every key/value pair, with correct `total` and pagination. (#27462)

  ```ts
  const skills = await storage.getStore('skills');
  await skills.listResolved({ metadata: { publishedBy: 'user-123' } });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/libsql@1.27.0, @mastra/pg@1.32.0_

- Fixed Slack channel messages so agents receive the text of Block Kit layout blocks (header, section, context, markdown), not just the notification fallback. Block-only messages now reach the agent. Thread history includes the block text too. (#27393)

- Fixed input processor rejections being ignored by agents running in separate worker processes. Upgrade both API and worker processes to apply the fix. (#27730)

- Fixed evented workflows losing recoverable snapshots when terminal notifications fail. (#27355)

- Fixed durable agent startup recovery getting stuck behind a stalled run. Each run's recovery wait is limited to 30 seconds without cancelling its execution, and recovery stops starting new work during shutdown. (#27357)

- Removed the `gray-matter` dependency. SKILL.md frontmatter is now parsed with the `yaml` package directly, which drops the vulnerable `sprintf-js` package (GHSA-hp3w-g68c-fv3c, no patched release available) from the production dependency tree. YAML frontmatter that worked before keeps working; JavaScript frontmatter (`---js`) is still rejected. (#27198)

- Fixed durable agent runs losing user input when a worker stops before the run finishes. (#27835)

  Durable runs now stop before calling the model when the accepted input cannot be saved — for example when the storage write fails or the caller has no write permission on the thread — instead of answering without persisting it. Memory configured with `readOnly` still runs without writing.

- Agent controller session routes no longer start a session as a side effect. Only `POST /agent-controller/:controllerId/sessions` starts one. Before, a message, model switch or state write that reached the server first created the session on a new random thread. The write then landed on a thread the client never read, so it looked lost. (#27474)

  Every other session route now acts on a started session. After a server restart it rebuilds the session from its stored thread. If the session was never started, the route returns `404` and creates nothing.

  ```ts
  const session = client.getAgentController('code').session(resourceId);
  await session.create({ threadId }); // start first
  await session.sendMessage('Hello'); // 404 if the session was never started
  ```

  `AgentController.createSession` accepts `resumeOnly: true` to get this behavior. It throws `SessionNotStartedError` when no stored thread matches. Factory now starts the session on its own thread when the session is created, so the first prompt can be sent right away.

  _Also in: @mastra/factory@0.24.0, @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Fixed paused tool calls picking up a different mode's tools when they resume. Approving, declining, or answering a paused tool call now uses the tools of the mode the run was paused in. Before, switching modes while a call waited gave the continuation the new mode's tools. A switch to an unrestricted mode could expose every tool to a run that started in a restricted one. The same applied when answering a suspension from another thread. Approving a plan still moves the run to the plan's transition mode. Fixes #27873. (#27911)

- Fixed `stopWhen` receiving the wrong step content when `savePerStep` is enabled. After a run was resumed (for example after a tool suspended to ask the user a question), a step could show up with no tool results, so conditions such as "stop after N failed tool calls" counted wrong and fired late. (#27521)

- Calling `abort()` with no reason from an output processor's `processOutputStream` now reports `Tripwire triggered by <processorId>` on durable and evented agents, the same as `Agent.stream()` and every other processor hook. Before this fix those engines reported `Stream part blocked by <processorId>`. (#27567)

- Fixed tool payload transforms not being applied to streamed tool calls. When a model streamed tool-call arguments, the `tool-call` chunk sent to the stream had no transform metadata, so UIs showed the raw arguments instead of the redacted display version. The stream now emits the model's final tool call, which carries the transform. (#27501)

  A tool call ID of `__proto__` no longer breaks the stream.

  A later step that reuses a tool call ID is now kept as its own call in `toolCalls` instead of being dropped.

- Fixed missing token usage when a tool call suspends or waits for approval. Suspended `stream()` and `generate()` results now include the step's tokens. After resuming, `totalUsage` stays cumulative and token metrics count those tokens once. See #27232. (#27425)

- Fixed messages being lost when sent to a thread shortly after the same instance cleaned up the thread's abandoned suspended run. Such a send used to return `deliver` while nothing received the message; it now returns `blocked`. Retry the send, and once the old run's lease expires (15 seconds by default) it starts a new run. (#27389)

- Added `AgentController.setThreadModel` to change a session thread's model without recreating the session. A running session bound to the thread switches immediately; otherwise the model is saved for its next run. (#27591)

  ```ts
  const { live } = await controller.setThreadModel({
    resourceId: 'session-1',
    threadId: 'thread-1',
    modelId: 'openai/gpt-5.6',
  });
  ```

- Fixed `isBadRequestError` retrying invalid API key errors. Newer OpenAI provider versions report `invalid_api_key` stream errors with status 400, so they were retried as bad requests instead of failing right away. Authorization errors now stay terminal. (#27788)

- Fixed trace, span and thread query validation errors for oversized membership sets. An `in`/`notIn` predicate with more than 100 values points at the set again (for example `where.set` or `where.spans.some.set`). Since the zod 4.6.7 upgrade, the error pointed only at `where`. (#27782)

- Improved cancellation of durable and evented agent tool and model calls after execution ownership is lost. Cooperative tools receive an abort signal so superseded work can stop sooner without canceling the recovering run. Recovery may run a tool again, so tools must be safe to repeat. Cancellation cannot undo work already completed. (#27490)

  Pass the tool's abort signal to operations that support cancellation:

  ```ts
  import { createTool } from '@mastra/core/tools';
  import { z } from 'zod';

  const fetchText = createTool({
    id: 'fetch-text',
    description: 'Fetch text from a URL.',
    inputSchema: z.object({ url: z.string().url() }),
    outputSchema: z.string(),
    execute: async ({ url }, context) => {
      // Without the signal, the request keeps running after ownership is lost.
      const response = await fetch(url, { signal: context?.abortSignal });
      return response.text();
    },
  });
  ```

- Fixed `timeTravel()` running the same steps twice when called concurrently on one run. `timeTravel()` now atomically claims the run before executing, like `resume()` does. A losing call throws `WORKFLOW_TIME_TRAVEL_ALREADY_CLAIMED` (or `WORKFLOW_TIME_TRAVEL_RUN_RUNNING` if the run is already executing) and `POST /api/workflows/:workflowId/time-travel-async` returns `409 Conflict`. The claim isn't enforced with storage adapters that don't support concurrent updates, when `shouldPersistSnapshot` excludes the `running` status, or in the Inngest runner; concurrent calls there can still run steps twice. Fixes #27279. (#27313)

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Fixed agents crashing with `Class constructor ... cannot be invoked without 'new'` when a tool's schema is a class-based Standard Schema, such as an Effect `Schema.Class` adapted with `Schema.toStandardSchemaV1`. Tools can now use class-based schemas the same way as `Schema.Struct`. (#27317)

- Fixed agents stopping after a server tool whose `execute` returns `undefined`. The tool now records a `null` result, and the agent includes the tool call and result in the next turn. (#27392)

- Fixed trace scoring so `type: 'agent'` scorers receive the tool calls an agent made. Scoring a stored agent trace (for example with `scoreTraceBatch` or `POST /api/observability/traces/score`) missed tool calls recorded under a model step. Tool calls and MCP tool calls are now included in the order they started. Calls made inside sub-agents or inside a tool are not credited to the parent agent. (#27390)

- Fixed `UnixSocketPubSub` intermittently failing to start a broker when the socket path is a bare relative name like `events.sock` or `./events.sock`. The broker's random private socket name could look like a number (for example `.28381e422682`), which Node treated as a TCP port and rejected `subscribe()` with `ERR_SOCKET_BAD_PORT` or `ENOENT`. The private socket path now always keeps its `./` prefix. (#27894)

- Fixed durable and evented agents losing a suspended run when an `untilIdle` stream is cancelled. Breaking out of `streamUntilIdle()` (or the request disconnecting) while the run waits for a tool approval or a `suspend()` resume now only stops reading, so `resumeStreamUntilIdle()` and `approveToolCall()` work afterwards instead of failing with "No registry entry found for run … Cannot resume". Cancelling while a tool is still running still aborts the run, and `result.abort()` still releases a suspended run. (#27563)

- Tools that require approval now validate their input before asking for approval. Previously an agent asked a person to approve a call whose arguments did not match the tool's `inputSchema`, and the call then failed validation after it was approved. Now the validation error goes straight back to the model so it can retry, and no `tool-call-approval` is emitted. This applies to `Agent`, durable agents, and agent networks. Fixes #26771. (#26973)

- Security: bump the internal `@ai-sdk/provider-utils-v6` alias from 4.0.40 to 4.0.56 to pick up the fix for AIKIDO-2026-526145. No public API changes. (#27023)

- Fixed deferred notifications running with no request context when the agent's delivery policy throws at delivery time. The delivery attempt now fails with the policy's error and is retried up to the attempt limit, instead of falling back to a bare wake that runs without a user. (#27729)

  Fixed low-priority notification summaries failing to save when the agent's memory depends on who owns the thread. The summary is still saved without starting a run, but now carries the delivery policy's request context.

- Fixed observation of unknown and completed durable agent runs so streams close promptly after replaying retained events, including when the requested offset skips the final event or a completed workflow snapshot remains after cleanup. Observers stay attached when lifecycle state cannot be read. (#27358)

- Fixed a message sent from an `AgentController` session being dropped when another agent was mid-run on the session's thread. The message now waits for that run to end and then runs. Pressing Stop while it waits drops it. If that run is waiting for an answer to its own prompt, the message isn't sent and the session shows an error saying so. A message sent while the thread is still held by a waiting run that was dropped from memory now waits for that hold to expire, then runs. Signals sent straight to the agent still report the thread as blocked. (#27624)

- Fixed goal judges and subagents hanging inside an Agent Controller session. Previously they inherited the session's memory thread and waited on it forever. Goal judges now use their own goal-specific thread. Forked subagents use the cloned thread. Non-forked subagents no longer inherit the session's thread and resource IDs. (#27323)

- Boot-time workflow recovery no longer re-runs a workflow run that another live server instance is still executing. Previously, when one replica started while another was mid-run, `restartAllActiveWorkflowRuns()` restarted every `running` run and its steps executed twice. (#27508)

  Workflow runs now hold the existing storage run-ownership lease (the one durable agents use) while they execute. `run.restart()` throws a `WORKFLOW_RESTART_RUN_OWNED` error instead of taking over a live run. The boot sweep skips those runs and checks them again once the lease could have expired, so runs left behind by a crashed process are still recovered. Storage adapters without run-ownership support keep the previous behavior.

### [@mastra/acp@0.4.3](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/acp@0.4.3/agent-sdks/acp/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed ACP prompts hanging when the agent stops with `max_tokens`, `cancelled`, `max_turn_requests` or `refusal`. `prompt()`, `AcpAgent.generate()` and ACP tools now reject with `ACP prompt stopped before completing: <reason>`. `AcpAgent.stream()` still returns a result, but its `text` promise rejects and reading `fullStream` errors with the same message. Reading only `fullStream` no longer leaves an unhandled rejection. (#27927)

### [@mastra/astra@1.1.2](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/astra@1.1.2/stores/astra/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Simplified vector inclusion options while preserving boolean request values. (#27646)

### [@mastra/clickhouse@1.26.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/clickhouse@1.26.0/stores/clickhouse/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added `timeToFirstChunkMs` to ClickHouse trace and span queries. `queryTraces()` returns the root span's time to first chunk and `querySpans()` returns each span's, both `null` when no chunk was streamed. You can also filter on it: (#27277)

  ```typescript
  where: { op: 'gt', left: { path: 'timeToFirstChunkMs' }, right: { literal: 1000 } }
  ```

- ClickHouse trace and span queries can now sort by `timeToFirstChunkMs`, slowest or fastest first. Traces and spans without a value come last. (#27473)

  ```typescript
  orderBy: [{ field: 'timeToFirstChunkMs', direction: 'desc' }];
  ```

- Added thread rows with previews, trace counts, activity timestamps, filters and cursor pagination. Legacy thread identity queries remain supported. (#27652)

  Broad thread queries exceeding the retained-history limit return a resource-limit error. Narrower time ranges can still succeed.

  ```ts
  import { planThreadRowsQuery } from '@mastra/core/storage';

  await storage.queryThreads(
    planThreadRowsQuery({
      timeRange: { traces: { from, to } },
    }),
  );
  ```

- Spans now store their time to first chunk in a new `timeToFirstChunkMs` column, ready for filtering and sorting by how fast a model started responding. Existing databases get the column automatically when the store starts. It stays empty for spans written before the upgrade or with an older `@mastra/core`. (#27140)

  _Also in: @mastra/duckdb@1.15.0, @mastra/pg@1.32.0_

- Added `querySpans()` support for context and parent/root entity fields, including thread filtering: (#27272)

  ```ts
  where: { op: 'eq', left: { path: 'threadId' }, right: { literal: 'thread-123' } }
  ```

  **Breaking with the updated core contract:** `querySpans()` no longer accepts `model` and `provider` filters or returns `cost`, `inputTruncated`, `outputTruncated` and `errorTruncated`.

  Reduced memory usage when querying spans and displaying previews for older data.

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed ClickHouse trace queries counting or grouping a trace by an outdated copy of its root span. When a run was exported more than once (for example after resuming), `aggregateTraces()` could count the same trace under both `status: error` and `status: success`, and `queryTraces()` could list a trace by a filter that only its older copy matched. Every trace read now uses the root copy that ended last, before applying filters. (#27679)

- Added support for grouping and filtering by the root span's `name` and `spanType` in the ClickHouse observability store. `aggregateTraces()` can group by both and count their distinct values, and `queryTraces()`, `aggregateTraces()`, and trace-query value discovery can filter on or suggest them. Values come from each trace's current completed root span. The store declares the `trace-query-root-span-fields` storage feature. (#27321)

  ```ts
  await mastraClient.aggregateTraces({ timeRange, groupBy: ['spanType', 'name'], measures: ['count'] });
  ```

### [@mastra/client-js@1.56.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/client-js@1.56.0/client-sdks/client-js/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- **Breaking:** `controller.session()` takes a session id, and the resource moves to `create()`. The `scope` argument is removed. (#27336)

  ```ts
  // Before
  const session = client.getAgentController('code').session('repo-1', 'run-a');
  await session.create();

  // After
  const session = client.getAgentController('code').session('run-a');
  await session.create({ resourceId: 'repo-1' });
  ```

  `create()` without a `resourceId` resumes the session on the resource it already has, or uses the session id as the resource for a new session, so `session('user-1').create()` behaves as before.

- Added experimental methods for MCP server events: (#27674)

  ```ts
  const { events } = await client.getMcpServerEvents('tickets');
  const { events: occurrences, cursor } = await client.pollMcpServerEvent('tickets', 'ticket.created', {
    arguments: { project: 'web' },
  });

  const controller = new AbortController();
  for await (const frame of client.streamMcpServerEvent(
    'tickets',
    'ticket.created',
    { cursor },
    { signal: controller.signal },
  )) {
    if (frame.type === 'event') console.log(frame.occurrence.data);
  }

  // Under `mastra dev` only
  await client.emitMcpServerEvent('tickets', 'ticket.created', { title: 'Test ticket' });
  ```

- Added thread rows, activity sorting and direct filters to `queryTraceThreads()`. (#27652)

  **Breaking type change:** `QueryTraceThreadsResult` now describes full thread rows. Use `QueryTraceThreadsResult<typeof request>` for legacy identity results.

  ```ts
  // Before
  await client.queryTraceThreads({ traces: { timeRange: { from, to } } });
  // After
  await client.queryTraceThreads({ timeRange: { traces: { from, to } } });
  ```

- Added context and parent/root entity fields to `querySpans()` response types. (#27272)

  **Breaking:** `querySpans()` response types no longer include `cost`, `inputTruncated`, `outputTruncated` and `errorTruncated`.

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed client tools failing with `ToolNotFoundError` after a tool approval. `approveToolCall`, `declineToolCall`, `approveToolCallGenerate` and `declineToolCallGenerate` now accept `clientTools` (and `clientToolsResolver`) and send them to the server, so the model can call a client tool after the approval decision. `approveToolCall` and `declineToolCall` also accept `memory`; pass the same thread and resource as the original call so client-tool results and the final answer are saved to that thread. (#27744)

  ```ts
  const stream = await agent.approveToolCall({
    runId,
    toolCallId,
    clientTools,
    memory: { thread: threadId, resource: resourceId },
  });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Added typed support for the new agent controller thread ownership route (`POST /agent-controller/:controllerId/sessions/:resourceId/threads/:threadId/owner`). (#27043)

  ```http
  POST /agent-controller/code/sessions/resource-1/threads/thread-1/owner
  → { "ok": true, "ownerId": "user-123" }
  ```

- Fixed a security alert when you install `@mastra/client-js`. The package no longer depends on `@ai-sdk/ui-utils`, which included a vulnerable version of `@ai-sdk/provider-utils`. Streaming works the same as before. (#27297)

- Fixed `approveToolCall`, `declineToolCall`, and `resumeStream` logging `tool_result must be preceded by a tool_call` on every resume. Client tools the model calls after a resumed tool call now run as expected. (#27207)

- Span types returned by the trace and span endpoints now include `timeToFirstChunkMs`, the milliseconds from span start to the first streamed chunk. (#27140)

- Agent controller session subscriptions now detect silent connections instead of hanging forever. When a stream receives no data (including server heartbeats) for `idleTimeoutMs` (default 60 seconds), it's treated as dropped, so `reconnect` and `onReconnect` re-establish it, or `onError` fires when reconnect is off. Opening a stream that never responds is bounded by the same limit. Set `idleTimeoutMs: 0` to disable. (#26904)

  ```ts
  const subscription = await session.subscribe({
    onEvent: handleEvent,
    onReconnect: resync,
    reconnect: true,
    idleTimeoutMs: 60_000,
  });
  ```

- Trace query requests (`queryTraces`, `aggregateTraces`, `querySpans`, `queryTraceThreads`) no longer retry when the server reports a query execution timeout (504 with code `TRACE_QUERY_EXECUTION_TIMEOUT`). Retrying re-ran the same expensive query against the same timeout and could make a failing call take over a minute. Other 5xx errors, including 504s from proxies or gateways, are still retried. (#27385)

- Added `modelListEditable` to agent details so clients can disable model-list editing when an agent resolves its models dynamically. Studio now shows function-valued model lists as read-only while keeping static model lists editable. (#27192)

  ```ts
  const agent = client.getAgent('weather-agent');
  const details = await agent.details();

  if (details.modelListEditable === false) {
    // Hide or disable model-list editing controls.
  }
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Trace queries, trace aggregates, and thread queries now return a 422 when they use the root span's `entityId` at trace scope and the configured observability store does not support it, instead of failing with a 500. For those stores, trace-query field discovery also omits trace-scope `entityId` from its field list and `aggregate.dimensions`. A new `traceQueryRootEntityId` capability reports whether the store supports it. Span-scope `entityId` inside `spans.some` keeps working on every store. (#27684)

  ```ts
  const { capabilities } = await mastraClient.getObservabilityCapabilities();
  if (capabilities.traceQueryRootEntityId) {
    await mastraClient.aggregateTraces({ timeRange, groupBy: ['entityId'], measures: ['count'] });
  }
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Trace queries, trace aggregates, and thread queries now return a 422 when they use the root span's `name` or `spanType` and the configured observability store does not support them, instead of failing with a 500. For those stores, trace-query field discovery also omits both fields from its field list and `aggregate.dimensions`. A new `traceQueryRootSpanFields` capability reports whether the store supports them. (#27321)

  ```ts
  const { capabilities } = await mastraClient.getObservabilityCapabilities();
  if (capabilities.traceQueryRootSpanFields) {
    await mastraClient.aggregateTraces({ timeRange, groupBy: ['name'], measures: ['count'] });
  }
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- The schedules HTTP API now accepts and returns `ifIdle.requireClaimedOwner` on agent schedules. Before this, `POST /schedules` and `PATCH /schedules/:scheduleId` dropped the flag, so a client that read a schedule and sent its `ifIdle` back cleared it. (#27896)

  ```ts
  await client.createSchedule({
    agentId: 'support',
    cron: '0 9 * * *',
    prompt: 'Morning check-in',
    threadId: 'thread-1',
    resourceId: 'user-1',
    ifIdle: { behavior: 'wake', requireClaimedOwner: true },
  });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Fixed client tools that run after `approveToolCall` / `declineToolCall` on an agent without memory. The follow-up model call now sees the whole conversation: the original user message, the approved (or declined) tool call and its result. Before, it saw only the client tool call and its result. (#27772)

  `approveToolCallGenerate` and `declineToolCallGenerate` now run executable client tools the model calls after the decision, and then continue the conversation, the same way `generate` does. Before, they returned with `finishReason: 'tool-calls'` and never ran the client tool.

  Pass `memory` to `approveToolCallGenerate` / `declineToolCallGenerate` to save that continuation to the thread:

  ```ts
  await agent.approveToolCallGenerate({
    runId,
    toolCallId,
    clientTools,
    memory: { thread: threadId, resource: resourceId },
  });
  ```

- The Agent Controller tool-approval endpoint now accepts `alwaysAllowCategory: true`, which approves the call and grants its tool category to the call's thread, the same way "always allow" works in an in-process session. Pass it from `@mastra/client-js` with `session.approveTool(toolCallId, true, { alwaysAllowCategory: true })`. (#27222)

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

- Added `timeToFirstChunkMs` to the `queryTraces()` and `querySpans()` result types, and the `traceQueryTimeToFirstChunk` flag to `getObservabilityCapabilities()`. (#27277)

  ```ts
  const { capabilities } = await mastraClient.getObservabilityCapabilities();

  if (capabilities.traceQueryTimeToFirstChunk) {
    const { spans } = await mastraClient.querySpans({
      timeRange,
      where: { op: 'gt', left: { path: 'timeToFirstChunkMs' }, right: { literal: 2000 } },
    });
  }
  ```

- Added `spanQueryContextFields` to observability capability response types. (#27807)

- `queryTraces()` and `querySpans()` now accept `timeToFirstChunkMs` in `orderBy`, and `getObservabilityCapabilities()` returns the `traceQueryTimeToFirstChunkSort` flag. (#27473)

  ```ts
  const { capabilities } = await mastraClient.getObservabilityCapabilities();

  if (capabilities.traceQueryTimeToFirstChunkSort) {
    const { spans } = await mastraClient.querySpans({
      timeRange,
      orderBy: [{ field: 'timeToFirstChunkMs', direction: 'desc' }],
    });
  }
  ```

### [@mastra/code-sdk@1.15.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/code-sdk@1.15.0/mastracode/sdk/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added an `outputProcessors` option to `createMastraCode`. Output processors you pass run after Mastra Code's built-in ones and before memory saves the reply, so changes they make to response messages are persisted. (#27162)

  ```ts
  const mastraCode = await createMastraCode({
    outputProcessors: ({ requestContext }) => [new ReplyLabelProcessor(requestContext)],
  });
  ```

- Added a Factory client for driving a remote Factory session. `RemoteSession` and `RemoteController` mirror a Factory session's state over its event stream and forward messages, approvals, and mode/model switches over HTTP. Platform helpers sign in to Mastra Platform and discover the organizations, projects, and environments to connect to. (#27222)

  ```ts
  import { createFactoryClient } from '@mastra/code-sdk/factory/client';
  import { RemoteSession } from '@mastra/code-sdk/factory/remote-session';
  import { getPlatformSession } from '@mastra/code-sdk/platform/index';

  const session = await getPlatformSession();
  const client = createFactoryClient({ url: 'https://my-factory.factory.mastra.cloud', session });
  ```

  Requests that fail with an expired Platform token refresh it once and retry.

- `SandboxFilesystem` now uses the sandbox's native `filesystem` when it has one. File reads, writes, stats, and listings take one request instead of two or three shell commands, and every file operation goes through it. `grep` and file walks still run as shell commands. Paths with `..` are still rejected. (#27859)

  On the native path, the in-sandbox symlink check is skipped by default, since an agent with a shell can read any file anyway. Set `verifySymlinks: true` when you serve files to people who can't run commands; each path is then checked once per instance:

  ```ts
  const fs = new SandboxFilesystem({ sandbox, workdir: '/workspace/repo', verifySymlinks: true });
  ```

- Added `resolveDefaultThinkingLevel` so a host can supply the thinking level for requests whose session has not picked one. When set, it replaces the settings.json defaults; a session's own level still wins. It runs on every model resolution, including account-rotation hops, so a changed default applies to the next request. (#27216)

  ```ts
  const { controller } = await createMastraCodeAgentController({
    resolveDefaultThinkingLevel: async ({ requestContext }) => lookUpTeamThinkingLevel(requestContext),
  });
  ```

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed ACP turns that an internal error ended returning `stopReason: "cancelled"` when the client never cancelled. `session/prompt` now returns a JSON-RPC error with the underlying message, so clients can tell a failure apart from a cancel. (#27438)

- Background task completions and notification wake-ups now find their session by session id, so they keep reaching the right session when several sessions share one resource. (#27336)

- Fixed the local trace store (`observability.duckdb`, used when `/observability local on`) growing without limit. Each session now deletes traces, logs and metrics older than 14 days and scores and feedback older than 30 days in the background, capped per session so startup is never blocked. (#27506)

- Fixed sandbox workspace search staying on the slow fallback for the whole session when its first search ran before the sandbox was ready. A failed search-tool check is now retried on the next search. (#27695)

- Credential resolution failures (for example a run with no signed-in user) are now logged as errors through the Mastra instance's logger, so they appear in deployed logs. The missing-user error also includes the session and thread ids and the names of the request context entries, so the code path that started the run can be identified. (#27376)

- Factory sessions now always use thread-scoped observational memory. Each run and chat keeps its own observations, and recall search still reaches every thread under the Factory or user resource. Session state, the `MASTRA_OM_SCOPE` environment variable, and `.mastracode/database.json` no longer change the scope for Factory sessions. Mastra Code outside Factory resolves the scope as before. (#27337)

- Fixed notifications for the thread you have open being treated as belonging to another project when the thread's recorded project path differs from the current checkout. Such notifications now run in your session instead of failing with "No model available". If a Mastra Code process for the thread's own project is also running, only one of the two delivers each notification. (#27634)

- Fixed "build a workflow" requests never finishing in Mastra Code. The workflow builder no longer inherits the chat session's memory thread, so it stops waiting on the session run that is waiting on it. (#27323)

- Added `normalizeLegacyProviderId` to `@mastra/code-sdk/providers/model-ids`. It maps a provider id Mastra Code once defined itself to the catalog id models.dev uses, so a reply's provider can be matched against the catalog. (#27162)

  ```ts
  import { normalizeLegacyProviderId } from '@mastra/code-sdk/providers/model-ids';

  normalizeLegacyProviderId('kimi-for-coding'); // 'kimi-code-plan-cn'
  normalizeLegacyProviderId('anthropic'); // 'anthropic'
  ```

- Fixed headless `--output jsonl` runs writing gigabytes of repeated data. The stream included a `display_state_changed` event after almost every other event, and each one carried a full copy of the session's display state, including every large tool result and all shell output so far. Long runs could fill the disk. (#26881)

  `--output jsonl` no longer writes `display_state_changed` events. Every other event, and the final `result` line, is written as before. The omitted snapshots only repeated what the other events already carry. Consumers that rebuilt UI state from these snapshots should fold the remaining events instead. `--output json`, `--output human`, and programmatic `runMC()` iteration are unchanged.

- Fixed a `prune() failed for domain "observability"` error being logged the first time local tracing opened a new `observability.duckdb` file. Mastra Code now skips the startup prune until the trace tables exist; the next session prunes as usual. (#27517)

- Auto memory now picks Gemini only when Mastra Code can use a Google credential for the request: a stored key or sign-in, or locally a `GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY` or `GOOGLE_API_KEY` environment variable. Deployed Factory accounts never use the server's environment keys, so they get a model from a provider they connected. (#26666)

- `/om` now shows the same Auto memory model that runs use. Before, the preview only checked `GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY`, so with `GOOGLE_API_KEY` or a Google key saved with `mastracode login` it showed Claude Haiku while runs used Gemini Flash. (#27449)

- Fixed foreground commands in Mastra Code to stop after 30 seconds unless the command specifies a timeout. Set timeout to 0 to disable the limit. Background processes are unaffected. (#26878)

- Improved content search in sandbox workspaces without ripgrep. When the sandbox's GNU grep and git support PCRE, patterns run with `grep -P` and `git grep -P`, so `\d`, `\w`, `\s`, `\b`, and lookarounds are searched in one command instead of falling back to reading files one by one. (#27698)

- Fixed headless runs reporting success while a tool was still waiting for an answer. When a tool such as `request_access` paused the run, `runMC` stopped with `status: 'completed'` and exit code `0`, so the answer from the permission policy was never applied. Headless runs now wait for the paused run to stop, send the policy's answer, and keep running until the resumed run finishes. If a paused tool can't be resumed, the run ends with `status: 'error'` and exit code `1` instead of reporting success. (#26883)

- Fixed headless runs cutting off their output when stdout is a pipe. The CLI exited before Node finished writing, so a large final result could reach the reader incomplete while the process still exited with code 0. Headless mode now waits for stdout and stderr to flush before exiting. If the output can't be delivered because the reader closed the pipe, a run that would otherwise succeed exits with code 1 instead of 0. A run that already failed keeps its exit code. (#26880)

- Improved sandbox grep reliability and diagnostics. When `rg` or `grep` fails inside the sandbox, the error now says why, for example a timeout or an exit code with the first stderr line. Previously every failure was reported as an unsupported pattern. The POSIX `grep` fallback now skips `.git`, and it keeps matches when only some files could not be read. (#27464)

- Scheduled prompts that fire into a session no longer held live in this process now run as that session's owner. Hosts that resume sessions (`resumeWakeSession`) bring the owner's session back first; if no owner can be found, the fire fails with `No session owns resource … to run its wake on thread …` before any run starts, instead of starting a run with no signed-in user that failed with "Tenant model credential policy could not be resolved: the request has no authenticated user." Only hosts with schedule tools enabled are affected. (#27780)

- Made content search in sandbox workspaces much faster when ripgrep isn't installed. Searches now run as a single GNU grep command with context lines, using git to respect every repository's `.gitignore`, instead of reading files one by one. With ripgrep, searches also respect `.gitignore` files now. Both skip `node_modules` unless the search path points inside it. File listings in sandbox workspaces also skip `node_modules` inside the sandbox instead of returning it to be filtered out. (#27687)

- Fixed the cross-agent signal sender telling the model to resend a signal that failed direct delivery. The signal waits in the recipient's inbox and is retried automatically, so the sender now tells the model not to resend it. A resend while that signal is still pending merges into it; a resend after it is delivered sends the recipient a second copy. (#27347)

- Added Extra high thinking to xAI models that list it, such as Grok 4.6. (#26530)

  ```ts
  import { getModelReasoningOptions } from '@mastra/core/llm';
  import { getAvailableThinkingLevelsForModel } from '@mastra/code-sdk/thinking';

  getAvailableThinkingLevelsForModel('xai/grok-4.6', getModelReasoningOptions('xai/grok-4.6'));
  // ['off', 'low', 'medium', 'high', 'xhigh']
  ```

- Mastra Code sessions no longer load web search providers, the Postgres store, the local trace store (DuckDB), the MCP client, local embeddings or voice SDKs until a feature needs them, which cuts startup memory by about 90 MB. (#27680)

- Added a `resumeWakeSession` option to `createMastraCode`. It runs when a notification is due on a thread whose session isn't open in this process, so the host can bring that session back online as its owner before the wake runs. If no session owns the thread afterwards, or the hook throws, the delivery fails with that error instead of waking with no user. (#27729)

  Pair it with `prepareWakeRequestContext`, which attaches the owner's identity to the wake itself:

  ```ts
  const code = await createMastraCode({
    resumeWakeSession: async ({ resourceId, threadId }) => {
      const owner = await lookupSessionOwner(resourceId);
      if (!owner) throw new Error(`Session ${resourceId} has no known owner`);
      const requestContext = new RequestContext([['user', owner]]);
      await code.controller.createSession({ id: resourceId, resourceId, ownerId: owner.id, threadId, requestContext });
    },
    prepareWakeRequestContext: async ({ requestContext, resourceId }) => {
      requestContext.set('user', await lookupSessionOwner(resourceId));
    },
  });
  ```

- Fixed woken notifications running in the wrong project when Mastra Code processes in different directories share one resource id. A due notification now runs as the session whose project the thread belongs to, and is delivered by a process running in that project: each project gets its own notification dispatch turn on the shared resource, so a process no longer runs another project's thread with its own workspace and tools, and no longer leaves it waiting until the process holding the resource exits. (#27426)

### [@mastra/connect@1.1.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/connect@1.1.0/packages/connect/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added Microsoft Teams as a supported Connect channel. When MASTRA_ENCRYPTION_KEY is unset, the Teams install store is encrypted with a key derived from the platform access token and a warning is logged; projects without a Teams connection are unaffected. (#27094)

- Added end-user (per-resource) connections to @mastra/connect. (#26923)

  **Scope tools to an end user**

  `tools()` accepts a new `resourceId` option — a static key or a callback derived per request — so an agent resolves the shared global connections plus that end user's own connections. Every platform call made under a resource scope asserts the key, so one user's connection can never serve another user's request.

  ```ts
  import { tools } from '@mastra/connect';

  // Per-request scoping on an agent:
  new Agent({ tools: ctx => tools({ resourceId: myUserIdFrom(ctx) }) });

  // Static/global usage is unchanged:
  new Agent({ tools: tools({ providers: ['linear'] }) });
  ```

  Calling `tools()` per request is cheap: identical options share one internal snapshot/MCP-client state, with per-resource caching and eviction. The returned resolver is also awaitable, so a dynamic `tools` argument can return it directly.

  **Let end users connect their own accounts**

  A new `userConnectRoutes()` export returns mountable API routes (provider catalog, the caller's connections, connect sessions, status polling, disconnect, and a hosted connect page) gated on the authenticated end user's resource key from `server.auth.mapUserToResourceId`.

  ```ts
  import { Mastra } from '@mastra/core';
  import { userConnectRoutes } from '@mastra/connect';

  export const mastra = new Mastra({
    server: {
      auth: {
        authenticateToken: async token => verifyToken(token),
        mapUserToResourceId: user => user.id,
      },
      apiRoutes: [...userConnectRoutes({ providers: ['linear', 'slack'] })],
    },
  });
  ```

  `credential()` also gains an optional `resourceId` option for asserting a resource-scoped connection's key.

- Added a callback form to tools() for per-request options. Pass a function that reads the request context and returns ToolsOptions to vary tool filters and approval policies per request (for example by user role), without losing the snapshot cache: resolutions producing the same options share one cached snapshot with the usual TTL semantics. (#27160)

  **Before** (per-request resolver, re-fetches every request):

  ```ts
  tools: async ctx => {
    const role = ctx.requestContext?.get('role');
    return tools({
      providers: {
        linear: role === 'viewer' ? { allowTools: ['linear_list_*'] } : { requireApproval: ['linear_delete_*'] },
      },
    })(ctx);
  };
  ```

  **After** (one resolver, cache intact):

  ```ts
  tools: tools(ctx => ({
    providers: {
      linear:
        ctx?.requestContext?.get('role') === 'viewer'
          ? { allowTools: ['linear_list_*'] }
          : { requireApproval: ['linear_delete_*'] },
    },
  }));
  ```

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed project-level Connect activity showing empty on the Mastra platform. @mastra/connect now sends its configured project id (MASTRA_PROJECT_ID or the projectId option) with every proxied provider request, so the platform can attribute tool and channel activity to the right project. (#26932)

  ```typescript
  import { tools } from '@mastra/connect';

  // Uses MASTRA_PROJECT_ID by default; pass projectId to override.
  const agentTools = tools({ projectId: 'proj_123' });
  ```

- **Google Drive** (#27156)

  - Fixed `google_drive_create_folder`, `google_drive_upload_document`, and `google_drive_move_file` failing with 404 errors on shared drives. Agents can now write to team shared drives.
  - Added `google_drive_export_file` for exporting Docs, Sheets, and Slides to PDF and other formats.
  - Added `google_drive_list_revisions` for listing a file's revision history.

  ```ts
  const toolset = await tools({ providers: ['google-drive'] })();

  await toolset.google_drive_export_file.execute({
    fileId: '1Kj3d86Z-Sfd56YP4dImQ-ggMRyP2QZ_BRO33zOO224c',
    mimeType: 'application/pdf',
  });
  ```

  **Fireflies**

  - Fixed `fireflies_get_transcript` failing validation on every transcript. Fireflies returns numeric `speaker_id`, `start_time`, and `end_time` values that the schema only accepted as strings; the tool now accepts and normalizes them.

- `@mastra/mcp` and `@mastra/connect` now require `@mastra/core` 1.79.0 or later. Upgrade `@mastra/core` together with them: (#27674)

  ```bash
  # Before: @mastra/core 1.78.x with an older @mastra/mcp
  npm install @mastra/mcp@latest # peer dependency error

  # After
  npm install @mastra/core@latest @mastra/mcp@latest @mastra/connect@latest
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/mcp@2.5.0_

### [@mastra/convex@1.7.5](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/convex@1.7.5/stores/convex/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed restarted `foreach` runs with nested workflows re-running already completed child steps when using Convex storage. (#26368)

### [@mastra/datadog@1.4.18](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/datadog@1.4.18/observability/datadog/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Simplified agentless-mode defaults while preserving configuration and environment handling. (#27646)

### [@mastra/deployer@1.79.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/deployer@1.79.0/packages/deployer/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed generated servers exiting immediately instead of shutting down gracefully on Ctrl-C under `mastra dev`, `mastra start`, or another tool that forwards the signal. The server receives Ctrl-C twice in a few milliseconds, once from the terminal and once from the parent, and treated the second as a request to force-quit. A second signal within 250ms of the first is now ignored, so in-flight requests drain and Mastra shuts down cleanly. Pressing Ctrl-C again after that still exits immediately. (#26986)

- Fixed `mastra build` and `mastra dev` failing in monorepos where multiple workspace packages share code. (#27204)

- Servers built with `mastra build` now start model usage telemetry before serving requests, so shutdown reports include every model call the server handled. (#26986)

- Fixed dev and build failures when projects use TypeScript 7. (#27840)

- Removed the `find-workspaces` and `gray-matter` dependencies. Monorepo workspace discovery now uses `tinyglobby` and markdown frontmatter is parsed by a helper shared with `@mastra/core`. This removes the vulnerable `braces` (GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm) and `sprintf-js` (GHSA-hp3w-g68c-fv3c) packages, neither of which has a patched release, from the production dependency tree of `@mastra/deployer`. `npm audit` no longer reports them for this package. (#27198)

- Fixed `mastra dev` failing when a `server.apiRoutes` handler or `bundler`/`deployer` option imports a workspace package that ships raw TypeScript. These packages are now inlined into the extracted config instead of being left as imports that Node cannot load. Their own dependencies still resolve from the workspace package, so dependencies installed only for that package (for example with pnpm) keep working. (#27303)

- Agent browser streaming now requires authentication. The browser WebSocket stream (`/browser/:agentId/stream`), the session probe, and the close endpoint previously accepted unauthenticated requests; they now require the same authentication as the rest of the server. (#26112)

  Requests without a valid credential for the server auth provider configured on `Mastra` are denied with `401`.

  Browser WebSocket upgrades authenticate with the session cookie that same-origin requests already send, so signed-in Studio sessions keep working. A WebSocket handshake cannot set an `Authorization` header, so a client that authenticates with a header first exchanges its credential for a single-use upgrade ticket over an authenticated HTTP request:

  ```ts
  const response = await fetch(`${apiUrl}/agents/my-agent/browser/stream-ticket`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ threadId }),
  });
  const { ticket } = await response.json();

  const ws = new WebSocket(`wss://example.com/browser/my-agent/stream?threadId=${threadId}&ticket=${ticket}`);
  ```

  The ticket is opaque, expires after 30 seconds, is bound to the agent and thread it was issued for, and is consumed by the first upgrade it authenticates. The credential is only ever presented to the mint endpoint, so it never reaches a proxy or an access log, and each connection attempt mints a fresh ticket.

  Issuance is bounded so the mint endpoint cannot be used to exhaust the server: each agent may request a limited number of tickets per minute and the process holds a capped number of unspent tickets, beyond which the endpoint answers `429` instead of allocating more.

  For clients that cannot call the mint endpoint, `?apiKey=` remains available as a handshake-only fallback:

  ```ts
  const ws = new WebSocket(`wss://example.com/browser/my-agent/stream?threadId=${threadId}&apiKey=${token}`);
  ```

  A token in a URL can be retained in proxy and access logs, so prefer the ticket flow, and pass a short-lived token if you use this fallback. The mint endpoint, the session probe, and the close endpoint also accept the token through the `Authorization` header.

  Studio's browser view follows the same rule: when Studio is configured with an `Authorization` header it requests an upgrade ticket for each connection, so a deployment that authenticates with a header keeps its live view instead of losing it to a `401`. Deployments that sign users in with a session cookie are unaffected.

  `setupBrowserStream` also accepts an optional `allowedOrigins` allowlist, in the same shape as Hono's `cors.origin`, and refuses an upgrade from any other origin with `403` before authentication runs. Upgrades from the server's own origin are always allowed, since a CORS allowlist names other origins and Studio is often served by the same server; this check compares the host and not the scheme. The deployer forwards `server.cors.origin` when it is explicitly configured.

  **Direct callers of `setupBrowserStream`:** the config now requires the `mastra` instance so the browser routes can be authenticated.

  Before:

  ```ts
  await setupBrowserStream(app, { getToolset });
  ```

  After:

  ```ts
  await setupBrowserStream(app, { mastra, getToolset });
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/hono@1.7.21_

- Fixed `mastra build` failing with `"<export>" cannot be exported from ".mastra/.build/<pkg>.mjs" as it is a reexport that references itself` when the app only reaches a workspace package through another workspace package, and those packages don't declare a `version`. (#27739)

- `mastra build` now succeeds when your app imports a private workspace package that has no `version` field. (#27057)

### [@mastra/deployer-netlify@1.2.38](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/deployer-netlify@1.2.38/deployers/netlify/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Netlify installs now apply `bundler.pnpmWorkspaceSettings` and packed workspace dependency `pnpmOverrides` to the generated pnpm workspace, for both edge and serverless targets. (#27708)

### [@mastra/docker@0.12.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/docker@0.12.0/workspaces/docker/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added build progress reporting to `DockerTemplate.build()`. Pass `onProgress` to receive step starts and completions (including cache hits), raw command output, and a `reused` event when an existing image is used. Failed results now include `failedStep`, the most recent `output` (capped by `maxOutputBytes`, default 64 KiB), and `outputTruncated`. `DockerSandbox` accepts `onTemplateBuildProgress` to follow template builds triggered by `start()`. (#26682)

  ```typescript
  const result = await template.build({
    onProgress: event => {
      if (event.type === 'step-start') console.log(event.step.name, event.step.cached ? '(cached)' : '');
    },
  });
  if (result.status === 'failed') console.error(result.failedStep?.name, result.output);
  ```

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed `kill()` on Podman. Before, `kill()` returned `false` and the process group kept running, so command timeouts, aborts and background process kills had no effect. The kill helper now attaches its output streams, which Podman's Docker-compatible API requires. Kills now stop the process group on both Docker and Podman. (#27292)

### [@mastra/duckdb@1.15.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/duckdb@1.15.0/stores/duckdb/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added `querySpans()` support for additional context filters and response fields, including thread filtering: (#27272)

  ```ts
  where: { op: 'eq', left: { path: 'threadId' }, right: { literal: 'thread-123' } }
  ```

  **Breaking with the updated core contract:** `querySpans()` no longer accepts `model` and `provider` filters or returns `cost`, `inputTruncated`, `outputTruncated` and `errorTruncated`.

  Parent/root entity type, ID and name, and `executionSource` return `null`. Equality filters on these fields match no spans.

- Added thread rows with previews, trace counts, activity timestamps, filters and cursor pagination. Legacy thread identity queries remain supported. (#27652)

  ```ts
  import { planThreadRowsQuery } from '@mastra/core/storage';

  await storage.queryThreads(
    planThreadRowsQuery({
      timeRange: { traces: { from, to } },
    }),
  );
  ```

  _Also in: @mastra/pg@1.32.0_

- Added time to first chunk to DuckDB trace and span queries. `queryTraces()` returns the root span's `timeToFirstChunkMs` and `querySpans()` returns each span's, or `null` when no chunk was streamed. Both accept `timeToFirstChunkMs` filters. (#27277)

  ```typescript
  where: { op: 'gt', left: { path: 'timeToFirstChunkMs' }, right: { literal: 2000 } }
  ```

- DuckDB trace and span queries can now sort by `timeToFirstChunkMs`, slowest or fastest first. Traces and spans without a value come last. (#27473)

  ```typescript
  orderBy: [{ field: 'timeToFirstChunkMs', direction: 'desc' }];
  ```

#### Patch Changes

- Added support for grouping and filtering by the root span's `name` and `spanType` in the DuckDB observability store. `aggregateTraces()` can group by both and count their distinct values, and `queryTraces()`, `aggregateTraces()`, and trace-query value discovery can filter on or suggest them. Values come from each trace's current completed root span. The store declares the `trace-query-root-span-fields` storage feature. (#27321)

  ```ts
  await mastraClient.aggregateTraces({ timeRange, groupBy: ['spanType', 'name'], measures: ['count'] });
  ```

### [@mastra/e2b@0.15.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/e2b@0.15.0/workspaces/e2b/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- `E2BSandbox.filesystem` reads and writes the sandbox's disk through E2B's files API. Each operation starts the sandbox if needed and retries once when E2B paused or killed it server-side. (#27859)

  ```ts
  const sandbox = new E2BSandbox();
  await sandbox.filesystem.writeFile('/home/user/notes.md', '# Notes');
  const entries = await sandbox.filesystem.readdir('/home/user');
  ```

  Copies, appends, and writes or moves with `overwrite: false` run as one command inside the VM, so they're atomic and file contents don't travel through your server.

### [@mastra/elysia@0.1.19](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/elysia@0.1.19/server-adapters/elysia/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed `/api/openapi.json` containing `$ref`s that point nowhere. Recursive (`z.lazy`) schemas and schemas tagged with `.meta({ id })` used to emit `#/definitions/...` or `#/$defs/...` refs that broke once inlined into the document. Those definitions now go into `components.schemas` and the refs point there, so tools like Swagger UI, swagger-parser, and OpenAPI client generators can load the spec. This covers both built-in and custom routes. When two different schemas share an id, the second one gets a suffixed name (for example `Invoice_2`) and neither overwrites the other. (#27745)

  _Also in: @mastra/server@1.79.0_

### [@mastra/evals@1.10.8](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/evals@1.10.8/packages/evals/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed the code tool-call accuracy scorer returning 0 in non-strict mode when `expectedToolOrder` contains the same tool more than once (for example `search → inspect → search`). Each expected tool now matches a separate call, and extra calls between matches are still allowed. (#27483)

- Fixed `noRedundantCalls` on trajectories built from traces. Two identical tool calls in a row are now flagged even when `processor_run` or `model_generation` steps sit between them, so scoring a run from its trace gives the same result as scoring it from its messages. (#27316)

- Fixed `blacklistedSequences` and `maxRetriesPerTool` on trajectories built from traces. Forbidden tool sequences and tool retries are now detected even when `processor_run` or `model_generation` steps sit between the tool calls, so scoring a run from its trace gives the same result as scoring it from its messages. (#27387)

- Fixed trajectory scorers giving wrong results on trajectories built from traces. Traces wrap tool calls in `model_generation` steps and put `processor_run` steps around them, so a correct run scored 0 for accuracy, `maxSteps` counted wrapper steps, and forbidden sequences and tool retries were missed. All trajectory checks (`compareTrajectories`, `checkTrajectoryEfficiency`, `checkTrajectoryBlacklist`, `analyzeToolFailures`, the code trajectory scorers, and the LLM trajectory judge) now read tool calls the same way for trace and message trajectories, so a run gets the same score either way. (#27487)

  Token budgets still read `model_generation` steps, and expectations that name `model_generation` or `processor_run` steps are still compared against the raw trajectory. `maxSteps` and the `index` in `redundantCalls` now count tool-level steps on trace trajectories.

  The new `toToolLevelSteps` helper returns this tool-level view if you need it in a custom scorer.

  ```ts
  import { toToolLevelSteps } from '@mastra/evals/scorers/utils';

  const toolNames = toToolLevelSteps(trajectory.steps).map(step => step.name);
  ```

  Retries are now grouped across `model_generation` steps of the same run, as they are on message trajectories. Tool calls in separate sub-agent runs are still counted separately.

### [@mastra/factory@0.24.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/factory@0.24.0/mastracode/factory/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Factory user sessions can now start sub-sessions and hear back from them. (#27103)

  - `factory_start_session` starts a side session with a prompt, or a role on a work item (like pressing **Start**, without moving the card). New sessions run as the caller, with the caller's credentials, and the caller follows the work item.
  - Spawned sessions report with `factory_message_origin`. A side session reports to the session that started it; a work-item session reports to everyone following the item. `failed` and `blocked` reports are urgent, `done` is high priority, and `progress` updates replace each other while unread.
  - Every run of a work session or side session ends with exactly one final report, however the run started (the board, a typed message, a wake, or after a restart): if the agent doesn't report, Factory sends `done`, `failed`, or `blocked` when the run ends, including when it stops waiting for someone to approve a tool call.
  - When a work session or side session holds a tool call for approval, whoever follows it gets an urgent `approval` notification right away and can approve or decline it with `factory_answer_approval`.
  - Followed items send a low-priority notice to running sessions when they change stage.
  - A `<factory-sessions>` state signal shows a session the side sessions it started and the work items it follows, once there is something to show.

- Moved the thinking level default onto each Factory. Organization admins set it under Settings → Models → Factory defaults, and every session and work-item run in that Factory uses it until it picks its own level. Every Factory, including existing ones, starts at medium after upgrading. (#27216)

  **Why:** the old default lived in the server's settings.json, so one value was shared by every Factory on the server, and with sign-in turned on it could only be changed after setting `MASTRACODE_DEPLOYMENT_ORGANIZATION_ID`.

  **Removed:** the `GET /web/config/thinking` and `PUT /web/config/thinking` routes and the `MASTRACODE_DEPLOYMENT_ORGANIZATION_ID` variable. Set the level on the Factory instead:

  ```bash
  # Before
  curl -X PUT "$FACTORY_URL/web/config/thinking" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{ "globalDefault": "high" }'

  # After
  curl -X PATCH "$FACTORY_URL/web/factory/projects/$FACTORY_ID" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{ "defaultThinkingLevel": "high" }'
  ```

  A level saved in settings.json (global or per mode) is no longer read by Factory sessions, so if you set one there, set it again on each Factory. The per-chat "Chat defaults" row is also gone; a level picked there still overrides the Factory default for the chats that used it, so run `/think default` in those chats to follow the Factory default.

- Treat linked Slack conversations as User Sessions, with interactive User Session tools authorized as the linked sender, including other participants in an organization-visible session. Private sessions remain owner-only. (#27685)

  Before: a linked teammate replying in a Slack session could not use User Session management tools without acting as the session owner. After: they can ask, for example, “Switch my other session to openai/gpt-6-luna,” and `factory_switch_session_model` checks their own session ownership rather than the Slack session owner's.

- Added a built-in MCP server to Factory at `/web/factory/projects/:projectId/mcp`. MCP clients such as ChatGPT can sign in with OAuth and use the Factory tools (work items, sessions, decisions, approvals, plus `search` and `fetch`) with the same permissions as the web UI. Factories on Mastra Platform auth sign users in through Platform automatically; other auth providers set `mcp.authorizationServers` (or `MASTRA_FACTORY_MCP_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER`). Pass `mcp: false` to turn it off. (#27228)

  ```ts
  new MastraFactory({
    // ...
    mcp: { authorizationServers: ['https://your-tenant.authkit.app'] },
  });
  ```

- Factory sessions now share memory. A Factory's work-item runs, side sessions, and Slack channel threads store their threads under the Factory id. The sessions a person opens, and their Slack DMs, store theirs under that person's user id, so a DM is never shared with the Factory's runs. Factory sessions always use thread-scoped observational memory, and session state, `MASTRA_OM_SCOPE`, and `.mastracod
